WEBVTT

1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:58,598
http://subscene.com/u/659433
Improved by: @Ivandrofly

2
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:02,078
Yellow, black. Yellow, black.
Yellow, black. Yellow, black.

3
00:01:02,103 --> 00:01:05,231
Ooh, black and yellow! Let's
shake it up a little.

4
00:01:16,326 --> 00:01:19,012
Barry! Breakfast is ready!

5
00:01:19,037 --> 00:01:20,371
Coming!

6
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:23,041
Hang on a second.

7
00:01:24,042 --> 00:01:25,101
Hello?

8
00:01:25,126 --> 00:01:26,269
- Barry?
- Adam?

9
00:01:26,294 --> 00:01:29,881
- Can you believe this is happening?
- I can't. I'll pick you up.

10
00:01:32,884 --> 00:01:34,219
Looking sharp.

11
00:01:37,931 --> 00:01:41,034
Use the stairs. Your father
paid good money for those.

12
00:01:41,059 --> 00:01:42,535
Sorry. I'm excited.

13
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,538
Here's the graduate. We're
very proud of you, son.

14
00:01:45,563 --> 00:01:48,041
A perfect report card, all B's.

15
00:01:48,066 --> 00:01:49,375
Very proud.

16
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:51,544
Ma! I got a thing going here.

17
00:01:51,569 --> 00:01:54,130
- You got lint on your fuzz.
- Ow! That's me!

18
00:01:54,155 --> 00:01:57,884
- Wave to us! We'll be in row 118,000.
- Bye!

19
00:01:57,909 --> 00:02:00,286
Barry, I told you, stop
flying in the house!

20
00:02:22,433 --> 00:02:23,993
- Hey, Adam.
- Hey, Barry.

21
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:27,455
- Is that fuzz gel?
- A little. Special day, graduation.

22
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:28,915
Never thought I'd make it.

23
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,584
Three days grade school,
three days high school.

24
00:02:31,609 --> 00:02:32,752
Those were awkward.

25
00:02:32,777 --> 00:02:37,006
Three days college. I'm glad I took a
day and hitchhiked around the hive.

26
00:02:37,031 --> 00:02:38,783
You did come back different.

27
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,426
Hi, Barry.

28
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:42,620
Artie, growing a mustache?
Looks good.

29
00:02:43,454 --> 00:02:45,431
- Hear about Frankie?
- Yeah.

30
00:02:45,456 --> 00:02:48,226
- You going to the funeral?
- No, I'm not going.

31
00:02:48,251 --> 00:02:50,395
Everybody knows, sting
someone, you die.

32
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:52,981
Don't waste it on a squirrel.
Such a hothead.

33
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:55,550
I guess he could have just
gotten out of the way.

34
00:03:03,391 --> 00:03:06,911
I love this incorporating an
amusement park into our day.

35
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:09,314
That's why we don't
need vacations.

36
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:18,823
Boy, quite a bit of pomp,
under the circumstances.

37
00:03:22,994 --> 00:03:25,346
- Well, Adam, today we are men.
- We are!

38
00:03:25,371 --> 00:03:26,723
- Bee-men.
- Amen!

39
00:03:26,748 --> 00:03:28,082
Hallelujah!

40
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:32,228
Students, faculty,
distinguished bees,

41
00:03:32,253 --> 00:03:34,839
please welcome Dean Buzzwell.

42
00:03:35,798 --> 00:03:40,345
Welcome, New Hive City
graduating class of

43
00:03:41,095 --> 00:03:42,513
9: 15.

44
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:45,808
That concludes our ceremonies.

45
00:03:47,435 --> 00:03:51,623
And begins your career
at Honex lndustries!

46
00:03:51,648 --> 00:03:53,249
Will we pick our job today?

47
00:03:53,274 --> 00:03:55,168
I heard it's just orientation.

48
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:57,195
Heads up! Here we go.

49
00:03:58,738 --> 00:04:02,325
Keep your hands and antennas
inside the tram at all times.

50
00:04:11,209 --> 00:04:14,212
- Wonder what it'll be like?
- A little scary.

51
00:04:15,421 --> 00:04:18,691
Welcome to Honex, a
division of Honesco

52
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:21,361
and a part of the Hexagon Group.

53
00:04:21,386 --> 00:04:22,845
This is it!

54
00:04:25,431 --> 00:04:26,599
Wow.

55
00:04:31,646 --> 00:04:32,872
Wow.

56
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:36,042
We know that you, as a bee,
have worked your whole life

57
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:40,213
to get to the point where you
can work for your whole life.

58
00:04:40,238 --> 00:04:44,592
Honey begins when our valiant Pollen
Jocks bring the nectar to the hive.

59
00:04:44,617 --> 00:04:46,761
Our top-secret formula

60
00:04:46,786 --> 00:04:51,057
is automatically color-corrected,
scent-adjusted and bubble-contoured

61
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:53,559
into this soothing sweet syrup

62
00:04:53,584 --> 00:04:57,505
with it's distinctive
golden glow you know as...

63
00:04:57,755 --> 00:04:59,924
Honey!

64
00:05:02,427 --> 00:05:04,737
- That girl was hot.
- She's my cousin!

65
00:05:04,762 --> 00:05:06,906
- She is?
- Yes, we're all cousins.

66
00:05:06,931 --> 00:05:10,785
- Right. You're right.
- At Honex, we constantly strive

67
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:13,997
to improve every aspect
of bee existence.

68
00:05:14,022 --> 00:05:17,608
These bees are stress-testing
a new helmet technology.

69
00:05:33,708 --> 00:05:36,102
- What do you think he makes?
- Not enough.

70
00:05:36,127 --> 00:05:39,856
Here we have our latest
advancement, the Krelman.

71
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:42,942
- What does that do?
- Catches that little strand of honey

72
00:05:42,967 --> 00:05:46,137
that hangs after you pour it.
Saves us millions.

73
00:05:48,973 --> 00:05:50,992
Can anyone work on the Krelman?

74
00:05:51,017 --> 00:05:54,954
Of course. Most bee jobs are
small ones. But bees know

75
00:05:54,979 --> 00:05:58,733
that every small job, if
it's done well, means a lot.

76
00:05:58,941 --> 00:06:00,293
But choose carefully

77
00:06:00,318 --> 00:06:05,323
because you'll stay in the job you
pick for the rest of your life.

78
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:09,969
The same job the rest of your
life? I didn't know that.

79
00:06:09,994 --> 00:06:11,704
What's the difference?

80
00:06:12,372 --> 00:06:16,684
You'll be happy to know that bees, as
a species, haven't had one day off

81
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:19,212
in 27 million years.

82
00:06:20,546 --> 00:06:22,357
So you'll just work us to death?

83
00:06:22,382 --> 00:06:23,800
We'll sure try.

84
00:06:41,609 --> 00:06:44,612
Wow! That blew my mind!

85
00:06:45,905 --> 00:06:48,508
"What's the difference?"
How can you say that?

86
00:06:48,533 --> 00:06:50,093
One job forever?

87
00:06:50,118 --> 00:06:51,886
That's an insane choice
to have to make.

88
00:06:51,911 --> 00:06:55,306
I'm relieved. Now we only have
to make one decision in life.

89
00:06:55,331 --> 00:06:58,351
But, Adam, how could they
never have told us that?

90
00:06:58,376 --> 00:07:00,978
Why would you question anything?
We're bees.

91
00:07:01,003 --> 00:07:03,673
We're the most perfectly
functioning society on Earth.

92
00:07:06,008 --> 00:07:09,654
You ever think maybe things
work a little too well here?

93
00:07:09,679 --> 00:07:11,556
Like what? Give me one example.

94
00:07:15,226 --> 00:07:17,895
I don't know. But you know
what I'm talking about.

95
00:07:21,274 --> 00:07:24,419
Please clear the gate. Royal
Nectar Force on approach.

96
00:07:24,444 --> 00:07:26,379
Wait a second. Check it out.

97
00:07:26,404 --> 00:07:28,698
- Hey, those are Pollen Jocks!
- Wow.

98
00:07:40,501 --> 00:07:42,437
I've never seen them this close.

99
00:07:42,462 --> 00:07:44,480
They know what it's
like outside the hive.

100
00:07:44,505 --> 00:07:46,691
Yeah, but some don't come back.

101
00:07:46,716 --> 00:07:48,301
- Hey, Jocks!
- Hi, Jocks!

102
00:08:11,449 --> 00:08:13,468
You guys did great!

103
00:08:13,493 --> 00:08:18,306
You're monsters! You're sky
freaks! I love it! I love it!

104
00:08:18,331 --> 00:08:20,600
- I wonder where they were.
- I don't know.

105
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:21,976
Their day's not planned.

106
00:08:22,001 --> 00:08:25,605
Outside the hive, flying who knows
where, doing who knows what.

107
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:28,274
You can't just decide
to be a Pollen Jock.

108
00:08:28,299 --> 00:08:29,984
You have to be bred for that.

109
00:08:30,009 --> 00:08:31,344
Right.

110
00:08:32,845 --> 00:08:36,282
Look. That's more pollen than you
and I will see in a lifetime.

111
00:08:36,307 --> 00:08:39,452
It's just a status symbol.
Bees make too much of it.

112
00:08:39,477 --> 00:08:45,191
Perhaps. Unless you're wearing it
and the ladies see you wearing it.

113
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:48,336
Those ladies? Aren't
they our cousins, too?

114
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:50,029
Distant. Distant.

115
00:08:51,113 --> 00:08:52,340
Look at these two.

116
00:08:52,365 --> 00:08:55,301
- Couple of Hive Harrys.
- Let's have fun with them.

117
00:08:55,326 --> 00:08:57,929
It must be dangerous
being a Pollen Jock.

118
00:08:57,954 --> 00:09:00,598
Yeah. Once a bear pinned
me against a mushroom!

119
00:09:00,623 --> 00:09:04,727
He had a paw on my throat, and with
the other, he was slapping me!

120
00:09:04,752 --> 00:09:07,188
- Oh, my!
- I never thought I'd knock him out.

121
00:09:07,213 --> 00:09:09,148
What were you doing during this?

122
00:09:09,173 --> 00:09:11,108
Trying to alert the authorities.

123
00:09:11,133 --> 00:09:12,735
I can autograph that.

124
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:15,530
A little gusty out there
today, wasn't it, comrades?

125
00:09:15,555 --> 00:09:16,989
Yeah. Gusty.

126
00:09:17,014 --> 00:09:20,493
We're hitting a sunflower patch
six miles from here tomorrow.

127
00:09:20,518 --> 00:09:22,328
- Six miles, huh?
- Barry!

128
00:09:22,353 --> 00:09:25,831
A puddle jump for us, but
maybe you're not up for it.

129
00:09:25,856 --> 00:09:27,792
- Maybe I am.
- You are not!

130
00:09:27,817 --> 00:09:30,403
We're going 0900 at J-Gate.

131
00:09:31,862 --> 00:09:35,199
What do you think, buzzy-boy?
Are you bee enough?

132
00:09:35,324 --> 00:09:39,495
I might be. It all depends
on what 0900 means.

133
00:09:49,171 --> 00:09:52,216
- Hey, Honex!
- Dad, you surprised me.

134
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,986
You decide what
you're interested in?

135
00:09:55,011 --> 00:09:58,472
- Well, there's a lot of choices.
- But you only get one.

136
00:09:59,056 --> 00:10:01,784
Do you ever get bored doing
the same job every day?

137
00:10:01,809 --> 00:10:03,869
Son, let me tell you
about stirring.

138
00:10:03,894 --> 00:10:07,790
You grab that stick, and you just move
it around, and you stir it around.

139
00:10:07,815 --> 00:10:11,085
You get yourself into a rhythm.
It's a beautiful thing.

140
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:13,629
You know, Dad, the
more I think about it,

141
00:10:13,654 --> 00:10:17,049
maybe the honey field
just isn't right for me.

142
00:10:17,074 --> 00:10:19,760
You were thinking of what,
making balloon animals?

143
00:10:19,785 --> 00:10:22,388
That's a bad job for a
guy with a stinger.

144
00:10:22,413 --> 00:10:25,683
Janet, your son's not sure
he wants to go into honey!

145
00:10:25,708 --> 00:10:29,562
- Barry, you are so funny sometimes.
- I'm not trying to be funny.

146
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:33,399
You're not funny! You're going
into honey. Our son, the stirrer!

147
00:10:33,424 --> 00:10:36,277
- You're gonna be a stirrer?
- No one's listening to me!

148
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:37,987
Wait till you see
the sticks I have.

149
00:10:38,012 --> 00:10:41,032
I could say anything right now.
I'm gonna get an ant tattoo!

150
00:10:41,057 --> 00:10:43,367
Let's open some honey
and celebrate!

151
00:10:43,392 --> 00:10:46,829
Maybe I'll pierce my thorax.
Shave my antennae.

152
00:10:46,854 --> 00:10:48,205
Shack up with a grasshopper.

153
00:10:48,230 --> 00:10:50,958
Get a gold tooth and
call everybody "dawg"!

154
00:10:50,983 --> 00:10:52,401
I'm so proud.

155
00:10:59,325 --> 00:11:02,219
- We're starting work today!
- Today's the day.

156
00:11:02,244 --> 00:11:04,305
Come on! All the good
jobs will be gone.

157
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:05,890
Yeah, right.

158
00:11:05,915 --> 00:11:10,895
Pollen counting, stunt bee, pouring,
stirrer, front desk, hair removal...

159
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:13,522
- Is it still available?
- Hang on. Two left!

160
00:11:13,547 --> 00:11:14,940
One of them's yours!

161
00:11:14,965 --> 00:11:16,717
Congratulations!
Step to the side.

162
00:11:17,468 --> 00:11:20,154
- What'd you get?
- Picking crud out. Stellar!

163
00:11:20,179 --> 00:11:21,280
Wow!

164
00:11:21,305 --> 00:11:22,531
Couple of newbies?

165
00:11:22,556 --> 00:11:24,825
Yes, sir! Our first day!
We are ready!

166
00:11:24,850 --> 00:11:26,394
Make your choice.

167
00:11:28,604 --> 00:11:30,915
- You want to go first?
- No, you go.

168
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:32,958
Oh, my. What's available?

169
00:11:32,983 --> 00:11:35,795
Restroom attendant's open, not
for the reason you think.

170
00:11:35,820 --> 00:11:39,532
- Any chance of getting the Krelman?
- Sure, you're on.

171
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:43,594
I'm sorry, the Krelman
just closed out.

172
00:11:43,619 --> 00:11:45,037
Wax monkey's always open.

173
00:11:46,038 --> 00:11:47,707
The Krelman opened up again.

174
00:11:47,832 --> 00:11:48,833
What happened?

175
00:11:49,041 --> 00:11:52,670
A bee died. Makes an opening.
See? He's dead. Another dead one.

176
00:11:52,795 --> 00:11:54,647
Deady. Deadified. Two more dead.

177
00:11:54,672 --> 00:11:58,109
Dead from the neck up. Dead from
the neck down. That's life!

178
00:11:58,134 --> 00:12:00,302
Oh, this is so hard!

179
00:12:01,178 --> 00:12:03,656
Heating, cooling, stunt
bee, pourer, stirrer,

180
00:12:03,681 --> 00:12:07,993
humming, inspector number seven,
lint coordinator, stripe supervisor,

181
00:12:08,018 --> 00:12:11,397
mite wrangler. Barry, what do
you think I should... Barry?

182
00:12:11,814 --> 00:12:13,149
Barry!

183
00:12:14,024 --> 00:12:18,045
All right, we've got the sunflower
patch in quadrant nine...

184
00:12:18,070 --> 00:12:19,714
What happened to you?
Where are you?

185
00:12:19,739 --> 00:12:22,091
- I'm going out.
- Out? Out where?

186
00:12:22,116 --> 00:12:23,676
- Out there.
- Oh, no!

187
00:12:23,701 --> 00:12:26,387
I have to, before I go to
work for the rest of my life.

188
00:12:26,412 --> 00:12:29,181
You're gonna die! You're crazy!
Hello?

189
00:12:29,206 --> 00:12:30,875
Another call coming in.

190
00:12:31,542 --> 00:12:35,813
If anyone's feeling brave,
there's a Korean deli on 83rd

191
00:12:35,838 --> 00:12:38,232
that gets their roses today.

192
00:12:38,257 --> 00:12:39,525
Hey, guys.

193
00:12:39,550 --> 00:12:42,737
- Look at that.
- Isn't that the kid we saw yesterday?

194
00:12:42,762 --> 00:12:45,030
Hold it, son, flight
deck's restricted.

195
00:12:45,055 --> 00:12:47,199
It's okay, Lou. We're
gonna take him up.

196
00:12:47,224 --> 00:12:49,101
Really? Feeling lucky, are you?

197
00:12:49,852 --> 00:12:52,705
Sign here, here.
Just initial that.

198
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:54,290
- Thank you.
- Okay.

199
00:12:54,315 --> 00:12:56,208
You got a rain advisory today,

200
00:12:56,233 --> 00:13:00,337
and as you all know,
bees cannot fly in rain.

201
00:13:00,362 --> 00:13:03,215
So be careful. As always,
watch your brooms,

202
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:06,761
hockey sticks, dogs,
birds, bears and bats.

203
00:13:06,786 --> 00:13:10,723
Also, I got a couple of reports
of root beer being poured on us.

204
00:13:10,748 --> 00:13:15,419
Murphy's in a home because of
it, babbling like a cicada!

205
00:13:15,586 --> 00:13:18,397
- That's awful.
- And a reminder for you rookies,

206
00:13:18,422 --> 00:13:22,568
bee law number one, absolutely
no talking to humans!

207
00:13:22,593 --> 00:13:24,762
All right, launch positions!

208
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:30,768
Buzz, buzz, buzz, buzz! Buzz, buzz,
buzz, buzz! Buzz, buzz, buzz, buzz!

209
00:13:31,977 --> 00:13:33,579
Black and yellow!

210
00:13:33,604 --> 00:13:34,772
Hello!

211
00:13:36,315 --> 00:13:38,167
You ready for this, hot shot?

212
00:13:38,192 --> 00:13:40,169
Yeah. Yeah, bring it on.

213
00:13:40,194 --> 00:13:41,587
Wind, check.

214
00:13:41,612 --> 00:13:43,672
- Antennae, check.
- Nectar pack, check.

215
00:13:43,697 --> 00:13:46,258
- Wings, check.
- Stinger, check.

216
00:13:46,283 --> 00:13:48,344
Scared out of my shorts, check.

217
00:13:48,369 --> 00:13:51,872
Okay, ladies, let's move it out!

218
00:14:01,298 --> 00:14:05,636
Pound those petunias, you
striped stem-suckers!

219
00:14:06,053 --> 00:14:10,307
All of you, drain those flowers!

220
00:14:26,532 --> 00:14:28,659
Wow! I'm out!

221
00:14:29,493 --> 00:14:31,412
I can't believe I'm out!

222
00:14:33,330 --> 00:14:34,999
So blue.

223
00:14:42,172 --> 00:14:44,508
I feel so fast and free!

224
00:14:46,010 --> 00:14:47,344
Box kite!

225
00:14:48,929 --> 00:14:50,014
Wow!

226
00:15:05,362 --> 00:15:07,448
Flowers!

227
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:14,847
This is Blue Leader.
We have roses visual.

228
00:15:14,872 --> 00:15:17,016
Bring it around 30° and hold.

229
00:15:17,041 --> 00:15:18,142
Roses!

230
00:15:18,167 --> 00:15:20,794
30°, roger. Bringing it around.

231
00:15:32,389 --> 00:15:34,892
Stand to the side, kid.
It's got a bit of a kick.

232
00:15:42,316 --> 00:15:44,026
That is one nectar collector!

233
00:15:45,194 --> 00:15:47,630
- Ever see pollination up close?
- No, sir.

234
00:15:47,655 --> 00:15:50,716
I pick up some pollen here,
sprinkle it over here.

235
00:15:50,741 --> 00:15:53,552
Maybe a dash over there,
a pinch on that one.

236
00:15:53,577 --> 00:15:55,846
See that? It's a
little bit of magic.

237
00:15:55,871 --> 00:15:58,223
That's amazing. Why
do we do that?

238
00:15:58,248 --> 00:15:59,808
That's pollen power.

239
00:15:59,833 --> 00:16:03,604
More pollen, more flowers, more
nectar, more honey for us.

240
00:16:03,629 --> 00:16:04,755
Cool.

241
00:16:25,901 --> 00:16:30,239
I'm picking up a lot of bright yellow.
Could be daisies. Don't we need those?

242
00:16:31,073 --> 00:16:32,424
Copy that visual.

243
00:16:32,449 --> 00:16:35,094
Wait. One of these flowers
seems to be on the move.

244
00:16:35,119 --> 00:16:37,621
Say again? You're reporting
a moving flower?

245
00:16:37,746 --> 00:16:39,081
Affirmative.

246
00:16:49,299 --> 00:16:51,235
That was on the line!

247
00:16:51,260 --> 00:16:53,904
This is the coolest. What is it?

248
00:16:53,929 --> 00:16:56,573
I don't know, but I'm
loving this color.

249
00:16:56,598 --> 00:17:00,744
It smells good. Not like
a flower, but I like it.

250
00:17:00,769 --> 00:17:02,413
Yeah, fuzzy.

251
00:17:02,438 --> 00:17:04,331
Chemical-y.

252
00:17:04,356 --> 00:17:06,525
Careful, guys. It's
a little grabby.

253
00:17:07,443 --> 00:17:09,795
My sweet lord of bees!

254
00:17:09,820 --> 00:17:11,822
Candy-brain, get off there!

255
00:17:12,114 --> 00:17:13,615
Problem!

256
00:17:16,326 --> 00:17:18,512
- Guys!
- This could be bad.

257
00:17:18,537 --> 00:17:19,747
Affirmative.

258
00:17:20,122 --> 00:17:21,457
Very close.

259
00:17:22,624 --> 00:17:23,959
Gonna hurt.

260
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:26,186
Mama's little boy.

261
00:17:26,211 --> 00:17:28,130
You are way out of
position, rookie!

262
00:17:31,133 --> 00:17:33,469
Coming in at you like a missile!

263
00:17:36,805 --> 00:17:39,016
Help me!

264
00:17:40,684 --> 00:17:42,494
I don't think these are flowers.

265
00:17:42,519 --> 00:17:44,955
- Should we tell him?
- I think he knows.

266
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:46,315
What is this?

267
00:17:51,028 --> 00:17:52,463
Match point!

268
00:17:52,488 --> 00:17:57,493
You can start packing up, honey,
because you're about to eat it!

269
00:18:06,168 --> 00:18:07,961
Yowser!

270
00:18:36,406 --> 00:18:37,866
Gross.

271
00:18:43,122 --> 00:18:44,581
There's a bee in the car!

272
00:18:46,959 --> 00:18:48,644
- Do something!
- I'm driving!

273
00:18:48,669 --> 00:18:50,562
- Hi, bee.
- He's back here!

274
00:18:50,587 --> 00:18:52,297
He's going to sting me!

275
00:19:00,347 --> 00:19:04,476
Nobody move. If you don't move,
he won't sting you. Freeze!

276
00:19:05,894 --> 00:19:07,246
He blinked!

277
00:19:07,271 --> 00:19:08,455
Spray him, Granny!

278
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:09,982
What are you doing?

279
00:19:18,073 --> 00:19:21,410
Wow, the tension level out
here is unbelievable.

280
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:24,079
I gotta get home.

281
00:19:33,964 --> 00:19:36,758
Can't fly in rain.
Can't fly in rain.

282
00:19:37,134 --> 00:19:38,510
Can't fly in rain.

283
00:19:47,644 --> 00:19:50,480
Mayday! Mayday! Bee going down!

284
00:19:53,692 --> 00:19:56,253
Ken, could you close
the window please?

285
00:19:56,278 --> 00:19:59,965
Check out my new rÃ©sumÃ©. I made
it into a fold-out brochure.

286
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:01,842
You see? Folds out.

287
00:20:01,867 --> 00:20:04,286
Oh, no. More humans.
I don't need this.

288
00:20:07,206 --> 00:20:08,849
What was that?

289
00:20:08,874 --> 00:20:12,502
Maybe this time. This time. This
time. This time! This time! This...

290
00:20:13,003 --> 00:20:14,897
Drapes!

291
00:20:14,922 --> 00:20:16,773
That is diabolical.

292
00:20:16,798 --> 00:20:20,027
It's fantastic. It's got
all my special skills,

293
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:21,778
even my top-ten favorite movies.

294
00:20:21,803 --> 00:20:23,697
What's your number one?
Star Wars?

295
00:20:23,722 --> 00:20:25,182
Nah, I don't go for that

296
00:20:27,142 --> 00:20:28,493
kind of stuff.

297
00:20:28,518 --> 00:20:31,413
No wonder we shouldn't talk to
them. They're out of their minds.

298
00:20:31,438 --> 00:20:33,707
When I leave a job interview,
they're flabbergasted,

299
00:20:33,732 --> 00:20:35,834
can't believe what I say.

300
00:20:35,859 --> 00:20:38,820
There's the sun. Maybe
that's a way out.

301
00:20:39,488 --> 00:20:42,532
I don't remember the sun
having a big 75 on it.

302
00:20:48,538 --> 00:20:51,308
I predicted global warming.

303
00:20:51,333 --> 00:20:55,045
I could feel it getting hotter. At
first I thought it was just me.

304
00:20:55,712 --> 00:20:57,881
Wait! Stop! Bee!

305
00:21:00,008 --> 00:21:02,886
Stand back. These
are winter boots.

306
00:21:05,764 --> 00:21:06,765
Wait!

307
00:21:07,182 --> 00:21:08,408
Don't kill him!

308
00:21:08,433 --> 00:21:11,620
You know I'm allergic to them!
This thing could kill me!

309
00:21:11,645 --> 00:21:14,398
Why does his life have
less value than yours?

310
00:21:14,856 --> 00:21:20,003
Why does his life have any less value
than mine? Is that your statement?

311
00:21:20,028 --> 00:21:22,464
I'm just saying all
life has value.

312
00:21:22,489 --> 00:21:25,033
You don't know what he's
capable of feeling.

313
00:21:27,035 --> 00:21:28,537
My brochure!

314
00:21:43,051 --> 00:21:44,970
There you go, little guy.

315
00:21:48,807 --> 00:21:51,660
I'm not scared of him.
It's an allergic thing.

316
00:21:51,685 --> 00:21:54,204
Put that on your
rÃ©sumÃ© brochure.

317
00:21:54,229 --> 00:21:56,206
My whole face could puff up.

318
00:21:56,231 --> 00:21:58,250
Make it one of your
special skills.

319
00:21:58,275 --> 00:22:01,236
Knocking someone out is
also a special skill.

320
00:22:04,573 --> 00:22:06,383
Right. Bye, Vanessa. Thanks.

321
00:22:06,408 --> 00:22:10,053
- Vanessa, next week? Yogurt night?
- Sure, Ken. You know, whatever.

322
00:22:10,078 --> 00:22:12,264
- You could put carob chips on there.
- Bye.

323
00:22:12,289 --> 00:22:14,583
- Supposed to be less calories.
- Bye.

324
00:22:21,214 --> 00:22:23,108
I gotta say something.

325
00:22:23,133 --> 00:22:26,303
She saved my life. I
gotta say something.

326
00:22:27,804 --> 00:22:29,389
All right, here it goes.

327
00:22:41,276 --> 00:22:42,444
Nah.

328
00:22:49,326 --> 00:22:51,161
What would I say?

329
00:22:53,455 --> 00:22:55,624
I could really get in trouble.

330
00:22:57,542 --> 00:23:01,104
It's a bee law. You're not
supposed to talk to a human.

331
00:23:01,129 --> 00:23:02,964
I can't believe I'm doing this.

332
00:23:03,465 --> 00:23:04,983
I've got to.

333
00:23:05,008 --> 00:23:07,302
Oh, I can't do it. Come on!

334
00:23:08,470 --> 00:23:10,322
No. Yes. No.

335
00:23:10,347 --> 00:23:12,349
Do it. I can't.

336
00:23:12,808 --> 00:23:17,204
How should I start it? "You
like jazz?" No, that's no good.

337
00:23:17,229 --> 00:23:19,731
Here she comes! Speak, you fool!

338
00:23:20,565 --> 00:23:21,958
Hi!

339
00:23:21,983 --> 00:23:23,710
I'm sorry.

340
00:23:23,735 --> 00:23:25,879
- You're talking.
- Yes, I know.

341
00:23:25,904 --> 00:23:27,464
You're talking!

342
00:23:27,489 --> 00:23:28,840
I'm so sorry.

343
00:23:28,865 --> 00:23:32,177
No, it's okay. It's fine.
I know I'm dreaming.

344
00:23:32,202 --> 00:23:34,346
But I don't recall going to bed.

345
00:23:34,371 --> 00:23:37,516
Well, I'm sure this is
very disconcerting.

346
00:23:37,541 --> 00:23:42,354
This is a bit of a surprise
to me. I mean, you're a bee!

347
00:23:42,379 --> 00:23:45,690
I am. And I'm not supposed
to be doing this,

348
00:23:45,715 --> 00:23:48,151
but they were all
trying to kill me.

349
00:23:48,176 --> 00:23:50,529
And if it wasn't for you...

350
00:23:50,554 --> 00:23:53,056
I had to thank you. It's
just how I was raised.

351
00:23:55,725 --> 00:23:57,285
That was a little weird.

352
00:23:57,310 --> 00:23:59,413
- I'm talking with a bee.
- Yeah.

353
00:23:59,438 --> 00:24:03,208
I'm talking to a bee. And
the bee is talking to me!

354
00:24:03,233 --> 00:24:06,086
I just want to say I'm grateful.
I'll leave now.

355
00:24:06,111 --> 00:24:09,589
- Wait! How did you learn to do that?
- What?

356
00:24:09,614 --> 00:24:12,008
The talking thing.

357
00:24:12,033 --> 00:24:16,096
Same way you did, I guess. "Mama,
Dada, honey." You pick it up.

358
00:24:16,121 --> 00:24:17,848
- That's very funny.
- Yeah.

359
00:24:17,873 --> 00:24:22,227
Bees are funny. If we didn't laugh, we'd
cry with what we have to deal with.

360
00:24:22,252 --> 00:24:23,353
Anyway...

361
00:24:23,378 --> 00:24:27,232
- Can I get you something?
- Like what?

362
00:24:27,257 --> 00:24:31,445
I don't know. I mean...
I don't know. Coffee?

363
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:33,822
I don't want to put you out.

364
00:24:33,847 --> 00:24:36,408
It's no trouble. It
takes two minutes.

365
00:24:36,433 --> 00:24:38,577
- It's just coffee.
- I hate to impose.

366
00:24:38,602 --> 00:24:41,455
- Don't be ridiculous!
- Actually, I would love a cup.

367
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:43,081
Hey, you want rum cake?

368
00:24:43,106 --> 00:24:44,583
- I shouldn't.
- Have some.

369
00:24:44,608 --> 00:24:46,710
- No, I can't.
- Come on!

370
00:24:46,735 --> 00:24:48,962
I'm trying to lose a
couple micrograms.

371
00:24:48,987 --> 00:24:51,089
- Where?
- These stripes don't help.

372
00:24:51,114 --> 00:24:52,257
You look great!

373
00:24:52,282 --> 00:24:55,260
I don't know if you know
anything about fashion.

374
00:24:55,285 --> 00:24:56,761
Are you all right?

375
00:24:56,786 --> 00:24:58,121
No.

376
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:04,019
He's making the tie in the cab
as they're flying up Madison.

377
00:25:04,044 --> 00:25:06,021
He finally gets there.

378
00:25:06,046 --> 00:25:09,774
He runs up the steps into the
church. The wedding is on.

379
00:25:09,799 --> 00:25:13,945
And he says, "Watermelon? I
thought you said Guatemalan."

380
00:25:13,970 --> 00:25:16,181
"Why would I marry
a watermelon?"

381
00:25:21,978 --> 00:25:24,623
Is that a bee joke?

382
00:25:24,648 --> 00:25:26,249
That's the kind of stuff we do.

383
00:25:26,274 --> 00:25:28,376
Yeah, different.

384
00:25:28,401 --> 00:25:31,379
So, what are you
gonna do, Barry?

385
00:25:31,404 --> 00:25:33,507
About work? I don't know.

386
00:25:33,532 --> 00:25:37,969
I want to do my part for the hive,
but I can't do it the way they want.

387
00:25:37,994 --> 00:25:39,763
I know how you feel.

388
00:25:39,788 --> 00:25:41,473
- You do?
- Sure.

389
00:25:41,498 --> 00:25:45,936
My parents wanted me to be a lawyer or a
doctor, but I wanted to be a florist.

390
00:25:45,961 --> 00:25:48,813
- Really?
- My only interest is flowers.

391
00:25:48,838 --> 00:25:52,509
Our new queen was just elected
with that same campaign slogan.

392
00:25:53,510 --> 00:25:56,154
Anyway, if you look...

393
00:25:56,179 --> 00:25:58,990
There's my hive right there.
See it?

394
00:25:59,015 --> 00:26:00,742
You're in Sheep Meadow!

395
00:26:00,767 --> 00:26:03,578
Yes! I'm right off
the Turtle Pond!

396
00:26:03,603 --> 00:26:08,500
No way! I know that area. I
lost a toe ring there once.

397
00:26:08,525 --> 00:26:11,211
- Why do girls put rings on their toes?
- Why not?

398
00:26:11,236 --> 00:26:14,756
- It's like putting a hat on your knee.
- Maybe I'll try that.

399
00:26:14,781 --> 00:26:18,176
- You all right, ma'am?
- Oh, yeah. Fine.

400
00:26:18,201 --> 00:26:20,579
Just having two cups of coffee!

401
00:26:33,758 --> 00:26:36,736
Anyway, this has been great.
Thanks for the coffee.

402
00:26:36,761 --> 00:26:38,113
Yeah, it's no trouble.

403
00:26:38,138 --> 00:26:41,975
Sorry I couldn't finish it. If I
did, I'd be up the rest of my life.

404
00:26:42,517 --> 00:26:43,727
Are you...

405
00:26:45,562 --> 00:26:47,664
Can I take a piece
of this with me?

406
00:26:47,689 --> 00:26:50,233
Sure! Here, have a crumb.

407
00:26:51,735 --> 00:26:53,361
- Thanks!
- Yeah.

408
00:26:55,030 --> 00:26:58,300
All right. Well, then, I
guess I'll see you around.

409
00:26:58,325 --> 00:26:59,826
Or not.

410
00:27:00,035 --> 00:27:01,720
Okay, Barry.

411
00:27:01,745 --> 00:27:04,764
And thank you so much
again, for before.

412
00:27:04,789 --> 00:27:06,808
Oh, that? That was nothing.

413
00:27:06,833 --> 00:27:09,586
Well, not nothing, but...
Anyway...

414
00:27:30,857 --> 00:27:32,667
This can't possibly work.

415
00:27:32,692 --> 00:27:35,337
He's all set to go. We
may as well try it.

416
00:27:35,362 --> 00:27:37,614
Okay, Dave, pull the chute.

417
00:27:41,034 --> 00:27:42,927
- Sounds amazing.
- It was amazing!

418
00:27:42,952 --> 00:27:45,847
It was the scariest,
happiest moment of my life.

419
00:27:45,872 --> 00:27:48,600
Humans! I can't believe
you were with humans!

420
00:27:48,625 --> 00:27:51,269
Giant, scary humans!
What were they like?

421
00:27:51,294 --> 00:27:53,521
Huge and crazy. They talk crazy.

422
00:27:53,546 --> 00:27:56,358
They eat crazy giant things.
They drive crazy.

423
00:27:56,383 --> 00:28:00,278
- Do they try and kill you, like on TV?
- Some of them. But some of them don't.

424
00:28:00,303 --> 00:28:01,905
- How'd you get back?
- Poodle.

425
00:28:01,930 --> 00:28:05,659
You did it, and I'm glad. You
saw whatever you wanted to see.

426
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:07,452
You had your "experience."

427
00:28:07,477 --> 00:28:10,622
Now you can pick out
your job and be normal.

428
00:28:10,647 --> 00:28:12,374
- Well...
- Well?

429
00:28:12,399 --> 00:28:13,625
Well, I met someone.

430
00:28:13,650 --> 00:28:16,378
You did? Was she Bee-ish?

431
00:28:16,403 --> 00:28:19,673
- A wasp? Your parents will kill you!
- No, no, no, not a wasp.

432
00:28:19,698 --> 00:28:21,883
- Spider?
- I'm not attracted to spiders.

433
00:28:21,908 --> 00:28:24,886
I know it's the hottest thing,
with the eight legs and all.

434
00:28:24,911 --> 00:28:27,138
I can't get by that face.

435
00:28:27,163 --> 00:28:29,265
So who is she?

436
00:28:29,290 --> 00:28:31,935
She's human.

437
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:36,481
No, no. That's a bee law. You
wouldn't break a bee law.

438
00:28:36,506 --> 00:28:38,483
- Her name's Vanessa.
- Oh, boy.

439
00:28:38,508 --> 00:28:41,361
She's so nice. And
she's a florist!

440
00:28:41,386 --> 00:28:44,197
Oh, no! You're dating
a human florist!

441
00:28:44,222 --> 00:28:45,532
We're not dating.

442
00:28:45,557 --> 00:28:49,953
You're flying outside the hive, talking
to humans that attack our homes

443
00:28:49,978 --> 00:28:54,040
with power washers and M-80s!
One-eighth a stick of dynamite!

444
00:28:54,065 --> 00:28:57,836
She saved my life! And
she understands me.

445
00:28:57,861 --> 00:28:59,045
This is over!

446
00:28:59,070 --> 00:29:00,613
Eat this.

447
00:29:00,864 --> 00:29:03,091
This is not over! What was that?

448
00:29:03,116 --> 00:29:05,969
- They call it a crumb.
- It was so stingin' stripey!

449
00:29:05,994 --> 00:29:09,514
And that's not what they eat. That's
what falls off what they eat!

450
00:29:09,539 --> 00:29:11,516
- You know what a Cinnabon is?
- No.

451
00:29:11,541 --> 00:29:14,978
It's bread and cinnamon and
frosting. They heat it up...

452
00:29:15,003 --> 00:29:16,104
Sit down!

453
00:29:16,129 --> 00:29:17,897
- Really hot!
- Listen to me!

454
00:29:17,922 --> 00:29:21,968
We are not them! We're us.
There's us and there's them!

455
00:29:22,218 --> 00:29:25,530
Yes, but who can deny the
heart that is yearning?

456
00:29:25,555 --> 00:29:28,366
There's no yearning. Stop
yearning. Listen to me!

457
00:29:28,391 --> 00:29:32,704
You have got to start thinking
bee, my friend. Thinking bee!

458
00:29:32,729 --> 00:29:34,539
- Thinking bee.
- Thinking bee.

459
00:29:34,564 --> 00:29:38,943
Thinking bee! Thinking bee!
Thinking bee! Thinking bee!

460
00:29:40,570 --> 00:29:42,964
There he is. He's in the pool.

461
00:29:42,989 --> 00:29:45,216
You know what your
problem is, Barry?

462
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:46,885
I gotta start thinking bee?

463
00:29:46,910 --> 00:29:49,053
How much longer will this go on?

464
00:29:49,078 --> 00:29:52,557
It's been three days!
Why aren't you working?

465
00:29:52,582 --> 00:29:55,226
I've got a lot of big life
decisions to think about.

466
00:29:55,251 --> 00:29:59,522
What life? You have no life! You
have no job. You're barely a bee!

467
00:29:59,547 --> 00:30:02,233
Would it kill you to
make a little honey?

468
00:30:02,258 --> 00:30:04,944
Barry, come out. Your
father's talking to you.

469
00:30:04,969 --> 00:30:06,905
Martin, would you talk to him?

470
00:30:06,930 --> 00:30:08,598
Barry, I'm talking to you!

471
00:30:15,438 --> 00:30:16,773
Sugar.

472
00:30:18,358 --> 00:30:20,026
Ah, honey, honey.

473
00:30:22,278 --> 00:30:25,882
You are my candy, girl.

474
00:30:25,907 --> 00:30:29,285
And you got me wanting you.

475
00:30:31,287 --> 00:30:32,622
Honey.

476
00:30:34,123 --> 00:30:36,292
Ah, sugar, sugar.

477
00:30:36,793 --> 00:30:38,561
You coming?

478
00:30:38,586 --> 00:30:40,421
Got everything?

479
00:30:40,630 --> 00:30:42,398
All set!

480
00:30:42,423 --> 00:30:43,942
Go ahead. I'll catch up.

481
00:30:43,967 --> 00:30:45,552
Don't be too long.

482
00:30:46,886 --> 00:30:50,949
L just can't believe the
loveliness of loving you

483
00:30:50,974 --> 00:30:54,227
I just can't believe it's true

484
00:30:54,811 --> 00:30:58,623
I just can't believe the
wonder of this feeling, too

485
00:30:58,648 --> 00:31:00,124
I just can't believe it's true.

486
00:31:00,149 --> 00:31:02,377
Watch this!

487
00:31:02,402 --> 00:31:03,862
Sugar.

488
00:31:05,321 --> 00:31:07,365
Ah, honey, honey

489
00:31:13,204 --> 00:31:14,873
Vanessa!

490
00:31:18,167 --> 00:31:21,187
- We're still here.
- I told you not to yell at him.

491
00:31:21,212 --> 00:31:22,981
He doesn't respond to yelling!

492
00:31:23,006 --> 00:31:25,316
- Then why yell at me?
- Because you don't listen!

493
00:31:25,341 --> 00:31:26,901
I'm not listening to this.

494
00:31:26,926 --> 00:31:28,319
Sorry, I've gotta go.

495
00:31:28,344 --> 00:31:30,780
- Where are you going?
- I'm meeting a friend.

496
00:31:30,805 --> 00:31:33,491
A girl? Is this why
you can't decide?

497
00:31:33,516 --> 00:31:34,659
Bye.

498
00:31:34,684 --> 00:31:36,978
I just hope she's Bee-ish.

499
00:31:40,857 --> 00:31:44,319
They have a huge parade of
flowers every year in Pasadena?

500
00:31:44,694 --> 00:31:49,173
To be in the Tournament of Roses,
that's every florist's dream!

501
00:31:49,198 --> 00:31:53,678
Up on a float, surrounded by
flowers, crowds cheering.

502
00:31:53,703 --> 00:31:57,123
A tournament. Do the roses
compete in athletic events?

503
00:31:57,540 --> 00:32:02,020
No. All right, I've got one. How
come you don't fly everywhere?

504
00:32:02,045 --> 00:32:05,356
It's exhausting. Why don't you
run everywhere? It's faster.

505
00:32:05,381 --> 00:32:08,192
Yeah, okay, I see, I see.
All right, your turn.

506
00:32:08,217 --> 00:32:11,696
TiVo. You can just freeze
live TV? That's insane!

507
00:32:11,721 --> 00:32:13,364
You don't have that?

508
00:32:13,389 --> 00:32:17,368
We have Hivo, but it's a disease.
It's a horrible, horrible disease.

509
00:32:17,393 --> 00:32:18,728
Oh, my.

510
00:32:19,479 --> 00:32:20,872
Dumb bees!

511
00:32:20,897 --> 00:32:24,125
You must want to sting
all those jerks.

512
00:32:24,150 --> 00:32:27,236
We try not to sting. It's
usually fatal for us.

513
00:32:27,528 --> 00:32:30,006
So you have to
watch your temper.

514
00:32:30,031 --> 00:32:33,384
Very carefully. You kick
a wall, take a walk,

515
00:32:33,409 --> 00:32:37,388
write an angry letter and throw it
out. Work through it like any emotion:

516
00:32:37,413 --> 00:32:40,041
Anger, jealousy, lust.

517
00:32:41,918 --> 00:32:44,354
Oh, my goodness! Are you okay?

518
00:32:44,379 --> 00:32:45,647
Yeah.

519
00:32:45,672 --> 00:32:48,024
- What is wrong with you?
- It's a bug.

520
00:32:48,049 --> 00:32:52,403
He's not bothering anybody.
Get out of here, you creep!

521
00:32:52,428 --> 00:32:54,656
What was that? A Pick
'n Save circular?

522
00:32:54,681 --> 00:32:57,075
Yeah, it was. How did you know?

523
00:32:57,100 --> 00:33:01,037
It felt like about 10 pages.
Seventy-five is pretty much our limit.

524
00:33:01,062 --> 00:33:03,122
You've really got that
down to a science.

525
00:33:03,147 --> 00:33:06,150
- I lost a cousin to Italian Vogue.
- I'll bet.

526
00:33:06,442 --> 00:33:09,487
What in the name of
Mighty Hercules is this?

527
00:33:09,988 --> 00:33:13,533
How did this get here?
Cute Bee, Golden Blossom,

528
00:33:13,741 --> 00:33:15,785
Ray Liotta Private Select?

529
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:19,305
- Is he that actor?
- I never heard of him.

530
00:33:19,330 --> 00:33:22,225
- Why is this here?
- For people. We eat it.

531
00:33:22,250 --> 00:33:24,435
You don't have enough
food of your own?

532
00:33:24,460 --> 00:33:27,105
- Well, yes.
- How do you get it?

533
00:33:27,130 --> 00:33:30,024
- Bees make it.
- I know who makes it!

534
00:33:30,049 --> 00:33:31,943
And it's hard to make it!

535
00:33:31,968 --> 00:33:35,780
There's heating, cooling, stirring.
You need a whole Krelman thing!

536
00:33:35,805 --> 00:33:38,074
- It's organic.
- It's our-ganic!

537
00:33:38,099 --> 00:33:39,450
It's just honey, Barry.

538
00:33:39,475 --> 00:33:41,119
Just what?

539
00:33:41,144 --> 00:33:44,956
Bees don't know about this! This
is stealing! A lot of stealing!

540
00:33:44,981 --> 00:33:49,152
You've taken our homes, schools,
hospitals! This is all we have!

541
00:33:49,736 --> 00:33:52,797
And it's on sale? I'm getting
to the bottom of this.

542
00:33:52,822 --> 00:33:54,699
I'm getting to the
bottom of all of this!

543
00:34:01,789 --> 00:34:03,291
Hey, Hector.

544
00:34:05,585 --> 00:34:07,920
- You almost done?
- Almost.

545
00:34:09,964 --> 00:34:12,175
He is here. I sense it.

546
00:34:21,350 --> 00:34:23,286
Well, I guess I'll go home now

547
00:34:23,311 --> 00:34:27,749
and just leave this nice honey
out, with no one around.

548
00:34:27,774 --> 00:34:29,609
You're busted, box boy!

549
00:34:30,485 --> 00:34:33,171
I knew I heard something.
So you can talk!

550
00:34:33,196 --> 00:34:36,132
I can talk. And now
you'll start talking!

551
00:34:36,157 --> 00:34:39,302
Where you getting the sweet
stuff? Who's your supplier?

552
00:34:39,327 --> 00:34:41,971
I don't understand. I
thought we were friends.

553
00:34:41,996 --> 00:34:44,791
The last thing we want
to do is upset bees!

554
00:34:45,374 --> 00:34:47,977
You're too late! It's ours now!

555
00:34:48,002 --> 00:34:50,480
You, sir, have crossed
the wrong sword!

556
00:34:50,505 --> 00:34:53,858
You, sir, will be lunch
for my iguana, Ignacio!

557
00:34:53,883 --> 00:34:56,010
Where is the honey coming from?

558
00:34:57,845 --> 00:34:59,447
Tell me where!

559
00:34:59,472 --> 00:35:02,016
Honey Farms! It comes
from Honey Farms!

560
00:35:25,748 --> 00:35:26,833
Crazy person!

561
00:36:02,493 --> 00:36:05,221
What horrible thing
has happened here?

562
00:36:05,246 --> 00:36:08,099
These faces, they never
knew what hit them.

563
00:36:08,124 --> 00:36:10,877
And now they're on
the road to nowhere!

564
00:36:11,794 --> 00:36:13,271
Just keep still.

565
00:36:13,296 --> 00:36:15,690
- What? You're not dead?
- Do I look dead?

566
00:36:15,715 --> 00:36:18,943
They will wipe anything that
moves. Where you headed?

567
00:36:18,968 --> 00:36:21,988
To Honey Farms. I am onto
something huge here.

568
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:26,742
I'm going to Alaska. Moose blood,
crazy stuff. Blows your head off!

569
00:36:26,767 --> 00:36:28,578
I'm going to Tacoma.

570
00:36:28,603 --> 00:36:30,746
- And you?
- He really is dead.

571
00:36:30,771 --> 00:36:32,081
All right.

572
00:36:32,106 --> 00:36:33,291
Uh-oh!

573
00:36:33,316 --> 00:36:35,376
- What is that?
- Oh, no!

574
00:36:35,401 --> 00:36:37,461
- A wiper! Triple blade!
- Triple blade?

575
00:36:37,486 --> 00:36:40,173
Jump on! It's your
only chance, bee!

576
00:36:40,198 --> 00:36:44,510
Why does everything have
to be so doggone clean?

577
00:36:44,535 --> 00:36:46,762
How much do you
people need to see?

578
00:36:46,787 --> 00:36:49,891
Open your eyes! Stick
your head out the window!

579
00:36:49,916 --> 00:36:53,144
From NPR News in Washington,
I'm Carl Kasell.

580
00:36:53,169 --> 00:36:55,755
But don't kill no more bugs!

581
00:36:57,924 --> 00:37:00,843
- Bee!
- Moose blood guy!

582
00:37:09,560 --> 00:37:11,204
- You hear something?
- Like what?

583
00:37:11,229 --> 00:37:12,997
Like tiny screaming.

584
00:37:13,022 --> 00:37:14,357
Turn off the radio.

585
00:37:26,118 --> 00:37:28,179
Whassup, bee boy?

586
00:37:28,204 --> 00:37:30,014
Hey, Blood.

587
00:37:30,039 --> 00:37:33,267
Just a row of honey jars, as
far as the eye could see.

588
00:37:33,292 --> 00:37:34,518
Wow!

589
00:37:34,543 --> 00:37:38,272
I assume wherever this truck goes
is where they're getting it.

590
00:37:38,297 --> 00:37:39,774
I mean, that honey's ours.

591
00:37:39,799 --> 00:37:42,568
- Bees hang tight.
- We're all jammed in.

592
00:37:42,593 --> 00:37:43,819
It's a close community.

593
00:37:43,844 --> 00:37:47,865
Not us, man. We on our own.
Every mosquito on his own.

594
00:37:47,890 --> 00:37:51,452
- What if you get in trouble?
- You a mosquito, you in trouble.

595
00:37:51,477 --> 00:37:54,956
Nobody likes us. They just smack.
See a mosquito, smack, smack!

596
00:37:54,981 --> 00:37:58,000
At least you're out in the
world. You must meet girls.

597
00:37:58,025 --> 00:38:02,338
Mosquito girls try to trade up,
get with a moth, dragonfly.

598
00:38:02,363 --> 00:38:04,490
Mosquito girl don't
want no mosquito.

599
00:38:05,866 --> 00:38:07,969
You got to be kidding me!

600
00:38:07,994 --> 00:38:11,580
Mooseblood's about to leave
the building! So long, bee!

601
00:38:11,998 --> 00:38:13,724
- Hey, guys!
- Mooseblood!

602
00:38:13,749 --> 00:38:17,837
I knew I'd catch y'all down here.
Did you bring your crazy straw?

603
00:38:34,395 --> 00:38:38,541
We throw it in jars, slap a label on
it, and it's pretty much pure profit.

604
00:38:38,566 --> 00:38:40,126
What is this place?

605
00:38:40,151 --> 00:38:42,503
A bee's got a brain the
size of a pinhead.

606
00:38:42,528 --> 00:38:44,030
They are pinheads!

607
00:38:45,406 --> 00:38:46,741
Pinhead.

608
00:38:47,408 --> 00:38:50,970
- Check out the new smoker.
- Oh, sweet. That's the one you want.

609
00:38:50,995 --> 00:38:52,638
The Thomas 3000!

610
00:38:52,663 --> 00:38:53,764
Smoker?

611
00:38:53,789 --> 00:38:58,210
Ninety puffs a minute, semi-automatic.
Twice the nicotine, all the tar.

612
00:39:00,046 --> 00:39:03,132
A couple breaths of this
knocks them right out.

613
00:39:05,926 --> 00:39:08,779
They make the honey,
and we make the money.

614
00:39:08,804 --> 00:39:11,349
"They make the honey,
and we make the money"?

615
00:39:31,869 --> 00:39:33,371
Oh, my!

616
00:39:36,332 --> 00:39:38,559
What's going on? Are you okay?

617
00:39:38,584 --> 00:39:40,644
Yeah. It doesn't last too long.

618
00:39:40,669 --> 00:39:44,315
Do you know you're in a
fake hive with fake walls?

619
00:39:44,340 --> 00:39:47,009
Our queen was moved here.
We had no choice.

620
00:39:47,385 --> 00:39:50,821
This is your queen? That's
a man in women's clothes!

621
00:39:50,846 --> 00:39:52,556
That's a drag queen!

622
00:39:57,311 --> 00:39:59,146
What is this?

623
00:40:08,072 --> 00:40:10,533
Oh, no! There's
hundreds of them!

624
00:40:21,335 --> 00:40:22,686
Bee honey.

625
00:40:22,711 --> 00:40:27,091
Our honey is being brazenly
stolen on a massive scale!

626
00:40:27,508 --> 00:40:30,194
This is worse than
anything bears have done!

627
00:40:30,219 --> 00:40:31,737
I intend to do something.

628
00:40:31,762 --> 00:40:32,905
Oh, Barry, stop.

629
00:40:32,930 --> 00:40:36,325
Who told you humans are taking
our honey? That's a rumor.

630
00:40:36,350 --> 00:40:38,160
Do these look like rumors?

631
00:40:38,185 --> 00:40:41,872
That's a conspiracy theory. These
are obviously doctored photos.

632
00:40:41,897 --> 00:40:44,166
How did you get
mixed up in this?

633
00:40:44,191 --> 00:40:45,751
He's been talking to humans.

634
00:40:45,776 --> 00:40:47,545
- What?
- Talking to humans?

635
00:40:47,570 --> 00:40:50,506
He has a human girlfriend.
And they make out!

636
00:40:50,531 --> 00:40:52,091
Make out? Barry!

637
00:40:52,116 --> 00:40:53,342
We do not.

638
00:40:53,367 --> 00:40:55,594
- You wish you could.
- Whose side are you on?

639
00:40:55,619 --> 00:40:56,887
The bees!

640
00:40:56,912 --> 00:41:01,767
I dated a cricket once in San Antonio.
Those crazy legs kept me up all night.

641
00:41:01,792 --> 00:41:04,562
Barry, this is what you
want to do with your life?

642
00:41:04,587 --> 00:41:08,399
I want to do it for all our lives.
Nobody works harder than bees!

643
00:41:08,424 --> 00:41:10,943
Dad, I remember you
coming home so overworked

644
00:41:10,968 --> 00:41:14,071
your hands were still stirring.
You couldn't stop.

645
00:41:14,096 --> 00:41:15,698
I remember that.

646
00:41:15,723 --> 00:41:19,577
What right do they have to our
honey? We live on two cups a year.

647
00:41:19,602 --> 00:41:22,371
They put it in lip balm
for no reason whatsoever!

648
00:41:22,396 --> 00:41:25,040
Even if it's true,
what can one bee do?

649
00:41:25,065 --> 00:41:26,876
Sting them where
it really hurts.

650
00:41:26,901 --> 00:41:28,210
In the face! The eye!

651
00:41:28,235 --> 00:41:29,503
- That would hurt.
- No.

652
00:41:29,528 --> 00:41:31,297
Up the nose? That's a killer.

653
00:41:31,322 --> 00:41:35,451
There's only one place you can sting the
humans, one place where it matters.

654
00:41:36,285 --> 00:41:40,873
Hive at Five, the hive's only
full-hour action news source.

655
00:41:45,711 --> 00:41:47,296
No more bee beards!

656
00:41:49,757 --> 00:41:52,234
With Bob Bumble at
the anchor desk.

657
00:41:52,259 --> 00:41:54,153
Weather with Storm Stinger.

658
00:41:54,178 --> 00:41:55,738
Sports with Buzz Larvi.

659
00:41:55,763 --> 00:41:57,765
And Jeanette Chung.

660
00:41:59,934 --> 00:42:02,745
- Good evening. I'm Bob Bumble.
- And I'm Jeanette Chung.

661
00:42:02,770 --> 00:42:05,581
A tri-county bee, Barry Benson,

662
00:42:05,606 --> 00:42:09,335
intends to sue the human
race for stealing our honey,

663
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:12,112
packaging it and profiting
from it illegally!

664
00:42:14,907 --> 00:42:17,468
Tomorrow night on
Bee Larry King,

665
00:42:17,493 --> 00:42:22,806
we'll have three former queens here in
our studio, discussing their new book,

666
00:42:22,831 --> 00:42:25,935
Classy Ladies, out
this week on Hexagon.

667
00:42:25,960 --> 00:42:28,145
Tonight we're talking
to Barry Benson.

668
00:42:28,170 --> 00:42:31,649
Did you ever think, "I'm a kid
from the hive. I can't do this"?

669
00:42:31,674 --> 00:42:34,193
Bees have never been afraid
to change the world.

670
00:42:34,218 --> 00:42:37,446
What about Bee Columbus?
Bee Gandhi? Bejesus?

671
00:42:37,471 --> 00:42:40,282
Where I'm from, we'd
never sue humans.

672
00:42:40,307 --> 00:42:43,452
We were thinking of
stickball or candy stores.

673
00:42:43,477 --> 00:42:44,870
How old are you?

674
00:42:44,895 --> 00:42:48,290
The bee community is
supporting you in this case,

675
00:42:48,315 --> 00:42:51,126
which will be the trial
of the bee century.

676
00:42:51,151 --> 00:42:54,296
You know, they have a Larry
King in the human world, too.

677
00:42:54,321 --> 00:42:56,657
It's a common name. Next week...

678
00:42:56,991 --> 00:43:01,679
He looks like you and has a show
and suspenders and colored dots...

679
00:43:01,704 --> 00:43:03,305
Next week...

680
00:43:03,330 --> 00:43:07,685
Glasses, quotes on the bottom from the
guest even though you just heard them.

681
00:43:07,710 --> 00:43:11,814
Bear Week next week! They're
scary, hairy and here live.

682
00:43:11,839 --> 00:43:16,802
Always leans forward, pointy
shoulders, squinty eyes, very Jewish.

683
00:43:18,512 --> 00:43:23,075
In tennis, you attack at
the point of weakness!

684
00:43:23,100 --> 00:43:26,996
It was my grandmother, Ken.
She's 81.

685
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:30,874
Honey, her backhand's a joke! I'm
not gonna take advantage of that?

686
00:43:30,899 --> 00:43:34,044
Quiet, please. Actual
work going on here.

687
00:43:34,069 --> 00:43:36,839
- Is that that same bee?
- Yes, it is!

688
00:43:36,864 --> 00:43:39,533
I'm helping him sue
the human race.

689
00:43:40,576 --> 00:43:42,886
- Hello.
- Hello, bee.

690
00:43:42,911 --> 00:43:44,179
This is Ken.

691
00:43:44,204 --> 00:43:47,433
Yeah, I remember you. Timberland,
size ten and a half.

692
00:43:47,458 --> 00:43:49,893
Vibram sole, I believe.

693
00:43:49,918 --> 00:43:51,895
Why does he talk again?

694
00:43:51,920 --> 00:43:54,857
Listen, you better go 'cause
we're really busy working.

695
00:43:54,882 --> 00:43:57,026
But it's our yogurt night!

696
00:43:57,051 --> 00:43:58,594
Bye-bye.

697
00:43:59,219 --> 00:44:02,139
Why is yogurt night
so difficult?

698
00:44:03,223 --> 00:44:06,910
You poor thing. You two have
been at this for hours!

699
00:44:06,935 --> 00:44:09,188
Yes, and Adam here has
been a huge help.

700
00:44:10,397 --> 00:44:12,708
- Frosting...
- How many sugars?

701
00:44:12,733 --> 00:44:14,943
Just one. I try not to
use the competition.

702
00:44:16,403 --> 00:44:18,280
So why are you helping me?

703
00:44:18,614 --> 00:44:20,741
Bees have good qualities.

704
00:44:22,159 --> 00:44:24,328
And it takes my
mind off the shop.

705
00:44:25,245 --> 00:44:28,682
Instead of flowers, people are
giving balloon bouquets now.

706
00:44:28,707 --> 00:44:31,060
Those are great,
if you're three.

707
00:44:31,085 --> 00:44:32,686
And artificial flowers.

708
00:44:32,711 --> 00:44:36,732
- Oh, those just get me psychotic!
- Yeah, me, too.

709
00:44:36,757 --> 00:44:39,193
Bent stingers,
pointless pollination.

710
00:44:39,218 --> 00:44:41,403
Bees must hate
those fake things!

711
00:44:41,428 --> 00:44:44,031
Nothing worse than a daffodil
that's had work done.

712
00:44:44,056 --> 00:44:47,284
Maybe this could make
up for it a little bit.

713
00:44:47,309 --> 00:44:50,287
- This lawsuit's a pretty big deal.
- I guess.

714
00:44:50,312 --> 00:44:52,581
You sure you want to
go through with it?

715
00:44:52,606 --> 00:44:55,542
Am I sure? When I'm done with the
humans, they won't be able to say,

716
00:44:55,567 --> 00:44:58,278
"Honey, I'm home," without
paying a royalty!

717
00:45:01,448 --> 00:45:04,468
It's an incredible scene
here in downtown Manhattan,

718
00:45:04,493 --> 00:45:09,098
where the world anxiously waits,
because for the first time in history,

719
00:45:09,123 --> 00:45:13,377
we will hear for ourselves if a
honeybee can actually speak.

720
00:45:17,965 --> 00:45:19,445
What have we gotten
into here, Barry?

721
00:45:21,343 --> 00:45:22,528
It's pretty big, isn't it?

722
00:45:22,553 --> 00:45:25,656
I can't believe how many humans
don't work during the day.

723
00:45:25,681 --> 00:45:29,810
You think billion-dollar multinational
food companies have good lawyers?

724
00:45:30,644 --> 00:45:33,647
Everybody needs to stay
behind the barricade.

725
00:45:45,367 --> 00:45:48,704
- What's the matter?
- I don't know, I just got a chill.

726
00:45:55,043 --> 00:45:57,980
Well, if it isn't the bee team.

727
00:45:58,005 --> 00:46:00,132
You boys work on this?

728
00:46:02,926 --> 00:46:06,655
All rise! The Honorable
Judge Bumbleton presiding.

729
00:46:06,680 --> 00:46:11,160
All right. Case number 4475,
Superior Court of New York,

730
00:46:11,185 --> 00:46:13,829
Barry Bee Benson versus
the Honey lndustry

731
00:46:13,854 --> 00:46:15,831
is now in session.

732
00:46:15,856 --> 00:46:20,502
Mr. Montgomery, you're representing
the five food companies collectively?

733
00:46:20,527 --> 00:46:22,171
A privilege.

734
00:46:22,196 --> 00:46:27,534
Mr. Benson, you're representing
all the bees of the world?

735
00:46:38,378 --> 00:46:41,632
I'm kidding. Yes, Your Honor,
we're ready to proceed.

736
00:46:43,175 --> 00:46:46,804
Mr. Montgomery, your
opening statement, please.

737
00:46:49,556 --> 00:46:52,201
Ladies and gentlemen
of the jury,

738
00:46:52,226 --> 00:46:55,370
my grandmother was
a simple woman.

739
00:46:55,395 --> 00:46:59,124
Born on a farm, she believed
it was man's divine right

740
00:46:59,149 --> 00:47:04,421
to benefit from the bounty
of nature God put before us.

741
00:47:04,446 --> 00:47:08,383
If we lived in the topsy-turvy
world Mr. Benson imagines,

742
00:47:08,408 --> 00:47:10,260
just think of what
would it mean.

743
00:47:10,285 --> 00:47:13,722
I would have to negotiate
with the silkworm

744
00:47:13,747 --> 00:47:17,100
for the elastic in my britches!

745
00:47:17,125 --> 00:47:19,061
Talking bee!

746
00:47:19,086 --> 00:47:21,605
How do we know this
isn't some sort of

747
00:47:21,630 --> 00:47:25,359
holographic motion-picture-capture
Hollywood wizardry?

748
00:47:25,384 --> 00:47:28,278
They could be using laser beams!

749
00:47:28,303 --> 00:47:31,448
Robotics! Ventriloquism!
Cloning!

750
00:47:31,473 --> 00:47:35,143
For all we know, he
could be on steroids!

751
00:47:37,479 --> 00:47:39,189
Mr. Benson?

752
00:47:44,945 --> 00:47:47,130
Ladies and gentlemen,
there's no trickery here.

753
00:47:47,155 --> 00:47:51,285
I'm just an ordinary bee.
Honey's pretty important to me.

754
00:47:51,618 --> 00:47:54,429
It's important to all bees.
We invented it!

755
00:47:54,454 --> 00:47:57,432
We make it. And we protect
it with our lives.

756
00:47:57,457 --> 00:47:59,893
Unfortunately, there are
some people in this room

757
00:47:59,918 --> 00:48:03,647
who think they can take it from
us 'cause we're the little guys!

758
00:48:03,672 --> 00:48:06,483
I'm hoping that, after
this is all over,

759
00:48:06,508 --> 00:48:10,946
you'll see how, by taking our honey,
you not only take everything we have,

760
00:48:10,971 --> 00:48:12,806
but everything we are!

761
00:48:16,351 --> 00:48:19,580
I wish he'd dress like that
all the time. So nice!

762
00:48:19,605 --> 00:48:21,373
Call your first witness.

763
00:48:21,398 --> 00:48:24,501
So, Mr. Klauss Vanderhayden
of Honey Farms,

764
00:48:24,526 --> 00:48:27,529
- big company you have.
- I suppose so.

765
00:48:27,654 --> 00:48:31,199
I see you also own
Honeyburton and Honron!

766
00:48:31,533 --> 00:48:34,344
Yes, they provide
beekeepers for our farms.

767
00:48:34,369 --> 00:48:37,973
Beekeeper. I find that to
be a very disturbing term.

768
00:48:37,998 --> 00:48:41,977
I don't imagine you employ
any bee-free-ers, do you?

769
00:48:42,002 --> 00:48:43,979
- No.
- I couldn't hear you.

770
00:48:44,004 --> 00:48:45,522
- No.
- No.

771
00:48:45,547 --> 00:48:49,359
Because you don't free bees.
You keep bees. Not only that,

772
00:48:49,384 --> 00:48:54,239
it seems you thought a bear would be an
appropriate image for a jar of honey.

773
00:48:54,264 --> 00:48:56,658
They're very lovable creatures.

774
00:48:56,683 --> 00:49:00,245
Yogi Bear, Fozzie
Bear, Build-A-Bear.

775
00:49:00,270 --> 00:49:01,605
You mean like this?

776
00:49:05,359 --> 00:49:07,194
Bears kill bees!

777
00:49:07,861 --> 00:49:11,006
How'd you like his head crashing
through your living room?

778
00:49:11,031 --> 00:49:14,076
Biting into your couch! Spitting
out your throw pillows!

779
00:49:15,285 --> 00:49:17,955
Okay, that's enough.
Take him away.

780
00:49:23,585 --> 00:49:28,065
So, Mr. Sting, thank you for being
here. Your name intrigues me.

781
00:49:28,090 --> 00:49:31,234
- Where have I heard it before?
- I was with a band called The Police.

782
00:49:31,259 --> 00:49:34,029
But you've never been a
police officer, have you?

783
00:49:34,054 --> 00:49:36,406
- No, I haven't.
- No, you haven't.

784
00:49:36,431 --> 00:49:38,951
And so here we have
yet another example

785
00:49:38,976 --> 00:49:41,787
of bee culture casually
stolen by a human

786
00:49:41,812 --> 00:49:44,289
for nothing more than a
prance-about stage name.

787
00:49:44,314 --> 00:49:45,540
Oh, please.

788
00:49:45,565 --> 00:49:47,751
Have you ever been
stung, Mr. Sting?

789
00:49:47,776 --> 00:49:50,712
Because I'm feeling a
little stung, Sting.

790
00:49:50,737 --> 00:49:55,033
Or should I say, Mr. Gordon M.
Sumner!

791
00:49:56,702 --> 00:49:59,454
That's not his real name?
You idiots!

792
00:50:00,372 --> 00:50:04,059
Mr. Liotta, first, belated
congratulations on your Emmy win

793
00:50:04,084 --> 00:50:07,020
for a guest spot on ER in 2005.

794
00:50:07,045 --> 00:50:08,922
Thank you. Thank you.

795
00:50:09,589 --> 00:50:12,776
I see from your rÃ©sumÃ© that
you're devilishly handsome

796
00:50:12,801 --> 00:50:16,013
with a churning inner turmoil
that's ready to blow.

797
00:50:16,304 --> 00:50:18,849
I enjoy what I do.
Is that a crime?

798
00:50:19,099 --> 00:50:22,160
Not yet it isn't. But is this
what it's come to for you?

799
00:50:22,185 --> 00:50:24,246
Exploiting tiny, helpless bees

800
00:50:24,271 --> 00:50:28,750
so you don't have to rehearse your
part and learn your lines, sir?

801
00:50:28,775 --> 00:50:31,336
Watch it, Benson! I
could blow right now!

802
00:50:31,361 --> 00:50:34,489
This isn't a goodfella.
This is a badfella!

803
00:50:35,657 --> 00:50:39,261
Why doesn't someone just step on
this creep, and we can all go home?

804
00:50:39,286 --> 00:50:42,014
- Order in this court!
- You're all thinking it!

805
00:50:42,039 --> 00:50:43,598
Order! Order, I say!

806
00:50:43,623 --> 00:50:45,959
- Say it!
- Mr. Liotta, please sit down!

807
00:50:51,506 --> 00:50:54,818
I think it was awfully nice of
that bear to pitch in like that.

808
00:50:54,843 --> 00:50:57,137
I think the jury's on our side.

809
00:50:57,971 --> 00:51:00,574
Are we doing everything
right, legally?

810
00:51:00,599 --> 00:51:01,825
I'm a florist.

811
00:51:01,850 --> 00:51:05,454
Right. Well, here's
to a great team.

812
00:51:05,479 --> 00:51:06,813
To a great team!

813
00:51:08,690 --> 00:51:10,500
Well, hello.

814
00:51:10,525 --> 00:51:12,502
- Ken!
- Hello.

815
00:51:12,527 --> 00:51:14,504
I didn't think you were coming.

816
00:51:14,529 --> 00:51:19,284
No, I was just late. I tried
to call, but... The battery.

817
00:51:19,826 --> 00:51:24,206
I didn't want all this to go to waste,
so I called Barry. Luckily, he was free.

818
00:51:24,831 --> 00:51:26,875
Oh, that was lucky.

819
00:51:28,585 --> 00:51:32,147
There's a little left.
I could heat it up.

820
00:51:32,172 --> 00:51:34,466
Yeah, heat it up,
sure, whatever.

821
00:51:38,678 --> 00:51:41,406
So, I hear you're quite
a tennis player.

822
00:51:41,431 --> 00:51:44,935
I'm not much for the game myself.
The ball's a little grabby.

823
00:51:47,187 --> 00:51:50,899
That's where I usually sit.
Right there.

824
00:51:52,609 --> 00:51:56,838
Ken, Barry was looking at your
rÃ©sumÃ©, and he agreed with me

825
00:51:56,863 --> 00:52:01,176
that eating with chopsticks
isn't really a special skill.

826
00:52:01,201 --> 00:52:04,262
You think I don't see
what you're doing?

827
00:52:04,287 --> 00:52:07,958
I know how hard it is to find the
right job. We have that in common.

828
00:52:08,708 --> 00:52:10,143
Do we?

829
00:52:10,168 --> 00:52:14,731
Bees have 100 percent employment, but
we do jobs like taking the crud out.

830
00:52:14,756 --> 00:52:17,526
That's just what I was
thinking about doing.

831
00:52:17,551 --> 00:52:22,556
Ken, I let Barry borrow your razor for
his fuzz. I hope that was all right.

832
00:52:26,810 --> 00:52:28,954
I'm going to drain
the old stinger.

833
00:52:28,979 --> 00:52:30,730
Yeah, you do that.

834
00:52:34,151 --> 00:52:35,819
Look at that.

835
00:52:44,578 --> 00:52:50,834
You know, I've just about had
it with your little mind games.

836
00:52:51,126 --> 00:52:53,478
- What's that?
- Italian Vogue.

837
00:52:53,503 --> 00:52:55,230
Mamma mia, that's
a lot of pages.

838
00:52:55,255 --> 00:52:56,648
A lot of ads.

839
00:52:56,673 --> 00:52:58,066
Remember what Van said,

840
00:52:58,091 --> 00:53:00,318
why is your life more
valuable than mine?

841
00:53:00,343 --> 00:53:03,471
Funny, I just can't
seem to recall that!

842
00:53:04,598 --> 00:53:07,492
I think something
stinks in here!

843
00:53:07,517 --> 00:53:09,077
I love the smell of flowers.

844
00:53:09,102 --> 00:53:12,247
How do you like the
smell of flames?

845
00:53:12,272 --> 00:53:13,315
Not as much.

846
00:53:29,206 --> 00:53:30,957
Water bug! Not taking sides!

847
00:53:33,001 --> 00:53:36,521
Ken, I'm wearing a Chapstick
hat! This is pathetic!

848
00:53:36,546 --> 00:53:38,256
I've got issues!

849
00:53:42,802 --> 00:53:45,864
Well, well, well, a royal flush!

850
00:53:45,889 --> 00:53:47,891
- You're bluffing.
- Am I?

851
00:53:58,652 --> 00:54:00,212
Surf's up, dude!

852
00:54:00,237 --> 00:54:01,488
Poo water!

853
00:54:04,908 --> 00:54:06,635
That bowl is gnarly.

854
00:54:06,660 --> 00:54:10,138
Except for those
dirty yellow rings!

855
00:54:10,163 --> 00:54:12,641
Kenneth! What are you doing?

856
00:54:12,666 --> 00:54:16,144
You know, I don't even like
honey! I don't eat it!

857
00:54:16,169 --> 00:54:18,230
We need to talk!

858
00:54:18,255 --> 00:54:20,148
He's just a little bee!

859
00:54:20,173 --> 00:54:25,153
And he happens to be the nicest
bee I've met in a long time!

860
00:54:25,178 --> 00:54:30,283
Long time? What are you talking about?
Are there other bugs in your life?

861
00:54:30,308 --> 00:54:33,703
No, but there are other
things bugging me in life.

862
00:54:33,728 --> 00:54:35,497
And you're one of them!

863
00:54:35,522 --> 00:54:38,708
Fine! Talking bees,
no yogurt night...

864
00:54:38,733 --> 00:54:42,921
My nerves are fried from riding
on this emotional roller coaster!

865
00:54:42,946 --> 00:54:44,698
Goodbye, Ken.

866
00:54:48,368 --> 00:54:50,178
And for your information,

867
00:54:50,203 --> 00:54:55,292
I prefer sugar-free, artificial
sweeteners made by man!

868
00:54:56,376 --> 00:54:58,853
I'm sorry about all that.

869
00:54:58,878 --> 00:55:02,549
I know it's got an aftertaste!
I like it!

870
00:55:04,384 --> 00:55:07,570
I always felt there was some kind
of barrier between Ken and me.

871
00:55:07,595 --> 00:55:10,282
I couldn't overcome it.
Oh, well.

872
00:55:10,307 --> 00:55:12,200
Are you okay for the trial?

873
00:55:12,225 --> 00:55:15,745
I believe Mr. Montgomery
is about out of ideas.

874
00:55:15,770 --> 00:55:19,791
We would like to call Mr. Barry
Benson Bee to the stand.

875
00:55:19,816 --> 00:55:21,209
Good idea!

876
00:55:21,234 --> 00:55:24,587
You can really see why he's
considered one of the best lawyers...

877
00:55:24,612 --> 00:55:25,612
Yeah.

878
00:55:28,116 --> 00:55:30,510
Layton, you've gotta weave
some magic with this jury,

879
00:55:30,535 --> 00:55:32,554
or it's gonna be all over.

880
00:55:32,579 --> 00:55:36,975
Don't worry. The only thing I have
to do to turn this jury around

881
00:55:37,000 --> 00:55:40,770
is to remind them of what
they don't like about bees.

882
00:55:40,795 --> 00:55:43,315
- You got the tweezers?
- Are you allergic?

883
00:55:43,340 --> 00:55:46,234
Only to losing, son.
Only to losing.

884
00:55:46,259 --> 00:55:51,072
Mr. Benson Bee, I'll ask you what
I think we'd all like to know.

885
00:55:51,097 --> 00:55:56,311
What exactly is your
relationship to that woman?

886
00:55:57,937 --> 00:55:58,955
We're friends.

887
00:55:58,980 --> 00:56:00,332
- Good friends?
- Yes.

888
00:56:00,357 --> 00:56:03,585
How good? Do you live together?

889
00:56:03,610 --> 00:56:06,780
Wait a minute...
Are you her little

890
00:56:07,280 --> 00:56:08,865
bedbug?

891
00:56:09,324 --> 00:56:12,969
I've seen a bee documentary or
two. From what I understand,

892
00:56:12,994 --> 00:56:16,848
doesn't your queen give birth
to all the bee children?

893
00:56:16,873 --> 00:56:19,976
- Yeah, but...
- So those aren't your real parents!

894
00:56:20,001 --> 00:56:21,811
- Oh, Barry...
- Yes, they are!

895
00:56:21,836 --> 00:56:23,146
Hold me back!

896
00:56:23,171 --> 00:56:26,232
You're an illegitimate
bee, aren't you, Benson?

897
00:56:26,257 --> 00:56:27,692
He's denouncing bees!

898
00:56:27,717 --> 00:56:30,362
Don't y'all date your cousins?

899
00:56:30,387 --> 00:56:33,139
- Objection!
- I'm going to pincushion this guy!

900
00:56:36,476 --> 00:56:38,144
Adam, don't! It's what he wants!

901
00:56:43,316 --> 00:56:45,627
Oh, I'm hit!

902
00:56:45,652 --> 00:56:47,796
Oh, lordy, I am hit!

903
00:56:47,821 --> 00:56:49,506
Order! Order!

904
00:56:49,531 --> 00:56:52,801
The venom! The venom is
coursing through my veins!

905
00:56:52,826 --> 00:56:56,846
I have been felled by a
winged beast of destruction!

906
00:56:56,871 --> 00:56:59,391
You see? You can't treat
them like equals!

907
00:56:59,416 --> 00:57:01,393
They're striped savages!

908
00:57:01,418 --> 00:57:06,005
Stinging's the only thing
they know! It's their way!

909
00:57:06,381 --> 00:57:09,134
- Adam, stay with me.
- I can't feel my legs.

910
00:57:10,343 --> 00:57:14,280
What angel of mercy will come
forward to suck the poison

911
00:57:14,305 --> 00:57:16,099
from my heaving buttocks?

912
00:57:16,766 --> 00:57:19,994
I will have order in my court.
Order!

913
00:57:20,019 --> 00:57:21,521
Order, please!

914
00:57:23,523 --> 00:57:26,376
The case of the honeybees
versus the human race

915
00:57:26,401 --> 00:57:28,962
took a pointed turn against
the bees yesterday

916
00:57:28,987 --> 00:57:32,824
when one of their legal team
stung Layton T. Montgomery.

917
00:57:47,547 --> 00:57:49,566
- Hey, buddy.
- Hey.

918
00:57:49,591 --> 00:57:51,342
- Is there much pain?
- Yeah.

919
00:57:53,887 --> 00:57:55,180
I...

920
00:57:56,014 --> 00:57:58,616
- I blew the whole case, didn't I?
- It doesn't matter.

921
00:57:58,641 --> 00:58:01,536
What matters is you're alive.
You could have died.

922
00:58:01,561 --> 00:58:04,372
I'd be better off dead.
Look at me.

923
00:58:04,397 --> 00:58:09,377
They got it from the cafeteria
downstairs, in a tuna sandwich.

924
00:58:09,402 --> 00:58:12,238
Look, there's a little
celery still on it.

925
00:58:13,072 --> 00:58:14,924
What was it like
to sting someone?

926
00:58:14,949 --> 00:58:17,385
I can't explain it.
It was all...

927
00:58:17,410 --> 00:58:20,914
All adrenaline and then...
And then ecstasy!

928
00:58:23,791 --> 00:58:25,268
All right.

929
00:58:25,293 --> 00:58:27,395
You think it was all a trap?

930
00:58:27,420 --> 00:58:30,690
Of course. I'm sorry. I
flew us right into this.

931
00:58:30,715 --> 00:58:32,692
What were we thinking?
Look at us.

932
00:58:32,717 --> 00:58:35,153
We're just a couple of
bugs in this world.

933
00:58:35,178 --> 00:58:37,572
What will the humans
do to us if they win?

934
00:58:37,597 --> 00:58:38,907
I don't know.

935
00:58:38,932 --> 00:58:42,410
I hear they put the roaches in
motels. That doesn't sound so bad.

936
00:58:42,435 --> 00:58:46,080
Adam, they check in, but
they don't check out!

937
00:58:46,105 --> 00:58:47,440
Oh, my.

938
00:58:49,984 --> 00:58:52,587
Could you get a nurse
to close that window?

939
00:58:52,612 --> 00:58:54,631
- Why?
- The smoke.

940
00:58:54,656 --> 00:58:56,282
Bees don't smoke.

941
00:58:56,908 --> 00:58:59,327
Right. Bees don't smoke.

942
00:59:00,453 --> 00:59:04,265
Bees don't smoke! But
some bees are smoking.

943
00:59:04,290 --> 00:59:06,643
That's it! That's our case!

944
00:59:06,668 --> 00:59:08,561
It is? It's not over?

945
00:59:08,586 --> 00:59:10,605
Get dressed. I've
gotta go somewhere.

946
00:59:10,630 --> 00:59:13,633
Get back to the court and stall.
Stall any way you can.

947
00:59:16,302 --> 00:59:19,781
And assuming you've
done step 29 correctly,

948
00:59:19,806 --> 00:59:20,974
you're ready for the tub.

949
00:59:26,896 --> 00:59:28,414
Mr. Flayman.

950
00:59:28,439 --> 00:59:29,999
Yes? Yes, Your Honor!

951
00:59:30,024 --> 00:59:32,794
Where is the rest of your team?

952
00:59:32,819 --> 00:59:35,213
Well, Your Honor,
it's interesting.

953
00:59:35,238 --> 00:59:37,966
Bees are trained to
fly haphazardly,

954
00:59:37,991 --> 00:59:40,702
and as a result, we don't
make very good time.

955
00:59:41,369 --> 00:59:43,638
I actually heard a
funny story about...

956
00:59:43,663 --> 00:59:46,349
Your Honor, haven't
these ridiculous bugs

957
00:59:46,374 --> 00:59:49,102
taken up enough of this
court's valuable time?

958
00:59:49,127 --> 00:59:53,898
How much longer will we allow these
absurd shenanigans to go on?

959
00:59:53,923 --> 00:59:58,444
They have presented no compelling
evidence to support their charges

960
00:59:58,469 --> 01:00:02,807
against my clients, who run
legitimate businesses.

961
01:00:03,182 --> 01:00:06,394
I move for a complete dismissal
of this entire case!

962
01:00:07,020 --> 01:00:08,830
Mr. Flayman, I'm
afraid I'm going

963
01:00:08,855 --> 01:00:11,291
to have to consider
Mr. Montgomery's motion.

964
01:00:11,316 --> 01:00:13,626
But you can't! We
have a terrific case.

965
01:00:13,651 --> 01:00:16,404
Where is your proof?
Where is the evidence?

966
01:00:16,529 --> 01:00:19,657
Show me the smoking gun!

967
01:00:20,199 --> 01:00:23,052
Hold it, Your Honor! You
want a smoking gun?

968
01:00:23,077 --> 01:00:25,413
Here is your smoking gun.

969
01:00:28,499 --> 01:00:30,059
What is that?

970
01:00:30,084 --> 01:00:31,603
It's a bee smoker!

971
01:00:31,628 --> 01:00:34,689
What, this? This harmless
little contraption?

972
01:00:34,714 --> 01:00:38,051
This couldn't hurt a
fly, let alone a bee.

973
01:00:44,390 --> 01:00:46,492
Look at what has happened

974
01:00:46,517 --> 01:00:50,747
to bees who have never been
asked, "Smoking or non?"

975
01:00:50,772 --> 01:00:52,915
Is this what nature
intended for us?

976
01:00:52,940 --> 01:00:55,877
To be forcibly addicted
to smoke machines

977
01:00:55,902 --> 01:00:58,379
in man-made wooden
slat work camps?

978
01:00:58,404 --> 01:01:03,117
Living out our lives as honey
slaves to the white man?

979
01:01:05,745 --> 01:01:09,040
- What are we gonna do?
- He's playing the species card.

980
01:01:09,582 --> 01:01:13,728
Ladies and gentlemen,
please, free these bees!

981
01:01:13,753 --> 01:01:17,023
Free the bees! Free the bees!

982
01:01:17,048 --> 01:01:18,566
Free the bees!

983
01:01:18,591 --> 01:01:21,569
Free the bees! Free the bees!

984
01:01:21,594 --> 01:01:25,573
The court finds in
favor of the bees!

985
01:01:25,598 --> 01:01:27,116
Vanessa, we won!

986
01:01:27,141 --> 01:01:30,144
I knew you could do it!
High-five!

987
01:01:31,187 --> 01:01:32,288
Sorry.

988
01:01:32,313 --> 01:01:35,416
I'm okay! You know
what this means?

989
01:01:35,441 --> 01:01:37,877
All the honey will finally
belong to the bees.

990
01:01:37,902 --> 01:01:40,321
Now we won't have to work
so hard all the time.

991
01:01:43,116 --> 01:01:47,762
This is an unholy perversion of
the balance of nature, Benson.

992
01:01:47,787 --> 01:01:49,455
You'll regret this.

993
01:01:50,623 --> 01:01:52,767
Barry, how much
honey is out there?

994
01:01:52,792 --> 01:01:54,644
All right. One at a time.

995
01:01:54,669 --> 01:01:56,437
Barry, who are you wearing?

996
01:01:56,462 --> 01:01:59,173
My sweater is Ralph Lauren,
and I have no pants.

997
01:01:59,674 --> 01:02:02,443
- What if Montgomery's right?
- What do you mean?

998
01:02:02,468 --> 01:02:06,597
We've been living the bee way a
long time, 27 million years.

999
01:02:08,349 --> 01:02:12,453
Congratulations on your victory. What
will you demand as a settlement?

1000
01:02:12,478 --> 01:02:16,190
First, we'll demand a complete
shutdown of all bee work camps.

1001
01:02:18,192 --> 01:02:21,212
Then we want back the honey
that was ours to begin with,

1002
01:02:21,237 --> 01:02:22,922
every last drop.

1003
01:02:22,947 --> 01:02:27,009
We demand an end to the glorification
of the bear as anything more

1004
01:02:27,034 --> 01:02:29,996
than a filthy, smelly,
bad-breath stink machine.

1005
01:02:30,329 --> 01:02:32,999
We're all aware of what
they do in the woods.

1006
01:02:34,000 --> 01:02:35,418
Wait for my signal.

1007
01:02:37,336 --> 01:02:38,546
Take him out.

1008
01:02:40,548 --> 01:02:43,484
He'll have nausea for a few
hours, then he'll be fine.

1009
01:02:43,509 --> 01:02:47,071
And we will no longer tolerate
bee-negative nicknames...

1010
01:02:47,096 --> 01:02:49,073
But it's just a
prance-about stage name!

1011
01:02:49,098 --> 01:02:52,493
Unnecessary inclusion of honey
in bogus health products

1012
01:02:52,518 --> 01:02:56,230
and la-dee-da human tea-time
snack garnishments.

1013
01:02:58,232 --> 01:02:59,525
Can't breathe.

1014
01:03:02,028 --> 01:03:03,571
Bring it in, boys!

1015
01:03:05,364 --> 01:03:07,700
Hold it right there! Good.

1016
01:03:09,243 --> 01:03:10,661
Tap it.

1017
01:03:14,457 --> 01:03:17,518
Mr. Buzzwell, we just
passed three cups,

1018
01:03:17,543 --> 01:03:19,228
and there's gallons more coming!

1019
01:03:19,253 --> 01:03:23,132
- I think we need to shut down!
- Shut down? We've never shut down.

1020
01:03:24,217 --> 01:03:28,429
Shut down honey production!
Stop making honey!

1021
01:03:33,100 --> 01:03:34,602
Turn your key, sir!

1022
01:03:46,239 --> 01:03:47,573
What do we do now?

1023
01:03:51,744 --> 01:03:53,412
Cannonball!

1024
01:04:01,420 --> 01:04:03,314
We're shutting down
honey production!

1025
01:04:03,339 --> 01:04:04,565
Mission abort.

1026
01:04:04,590 --> 01:04:08,094
Aborting pollination and nectar
detail. Returning to base.

1027
01:04:55,850 --> 01:04:58,661
Adam, you wouldn't believe how
much honey was out there.

1028
01:04:58,686 --> 01:04:59,704
Oh, yeah?

1029
01:04:59,729 --> 01:05:02,481
What's going on?
Where is everybody?

1030
01:05:03,566 --> 01:05:05,877
- Are they out celebrating?
- They're home.

1031
01:05:05,902 --> 01:05:09,213
They don't know what to do.
Laying out, sleeping in.

1032
01:05:09,238 --> 01:05:12,383
I heard your Uncle Carl was on his
way to San Antonio with a cricket.

1033
01:05:12,408 --> 01:05:13,968
At least we got our honey back.

1034
01:05:13,993 --> 01:05:18,222
Sometimes I think, so what if humans
liked our honey? Who wouldn't?

1035
01:05:18,247 --> 01:05:22,710
It's the greatest thing in the world! I
was excited to be part of making it.

1036
01:05:23,836 --> 01:05:26,856
This was my new desk.
This was my new job.

1037
01:05:26,881 --> 01:05:29,342
I wanted to do it really well.
And now...

1038
01:05:30,676 --> 01:05:32,345
Now I can't.

1039
01:05:42,980 --> 01:05:45,917
I don't understand why
they're not happy.

1040
01:05:45,942 --> 01:05:48,210
I thought their lives
would be better!

1041
01:05:48,235 --> 01:05:51,923
They're doing nothing. It's amazing.
Honey really changes people.

1042
01:05:51,948 --> 01:05:55,051
You don't have any idea
what's going on, do you?

1043
01:05:55,076 --> 01:05:57,745
- What did you want to show me?
- This.

1044
01:05:59,747 --> 01:06:01,098
What happened here?

1045
01:06:01,123 --> 01:06:03,417
That is not the half of it.

1046
01:06:09,674 --> 01:06:11,676
Oh, no. Oh, my.

1047
01:06:12,551 --> 01:06:14,445
They're all wilting.

1048
01:06:14,470 --> 01:06:16,614
Doesn't look very good, does it?

1049
01:06:16,639 --> 01:06:18,032
No.

1050
01:06:18,057 --> 01:06:20,768
And whose fault do
you think that is?

1051
01:06:23,396 --> 01:06:24,747
You know, I'm gonna guess bees.

1052
01:06:24,772 --> 01:06:28,376
- Bees?
- Specifically, me.

1053
01:06:28,401 --> 01:06:32,630
I didn't think bees not needing to make
honey would affect all these things.

1054
01:06:32,655 --> 01:06:37,718
It's not just flowers. Fruits,
vegetables, they all need bees.

1055
01:06:37,743 --> 01:06:40,638
That's our whole SAT
test right there.

1056
01:06:40,663 --> 01:06:44,266
Take away produce, that affects
the entire animal kingdom.

1057
01:06:44,291 --> 01:06:48,170
And then, of course...
The human species?

1058
01:06:50,798 --> 01:06:53,234
So if there's no
more pollination,

1059
01:06:53,259 --> 01:06:55,778
it could all just go
south here, couldn't it?

1060
01:06:55,803 --> 01:06:59,140
I know this is also
partly my fault.

1061
01:07:03,269 --> 01:07:05,454
How about a suicide pact?

1062
01:07:05,479 --> 01:07:06,956
How do we do it?

1063
01:07:06,981 --> 01:07:10,126
- I'll sting you, you step on me.
- That just kills you twice.

1064
01:07:10,151 --> 01:07:11,485
Right, right.

1065
01:07:12,111 --> 01:07:16,198
Listen, Barry, sorry,
but I gotta get going.

1066
01:07:23,164 --> 01:07:25,666
I had to open my mouth and talk.

1067
01:07:28,294 --> 01:07:29,628
Vanessa?

1068
01:07:32,465 --> 01:07:35,609
Vanessa? Why are you leaving?
Where are you going?

1069
01:07:35,634 --> 01:07:38,696
To the final Tournament of
Roses parade in Pasadena.

1070
01:07:38,721 --> 01:07:42,700
They've moved it to this weekend
because all the flowers are dying.

1071
01:07:42,725 --> 01:07:46,203
It's the last chance I'll
ever have to see it.

1072
01:07:46,228 --> 01:07:50,750
Vanessa, I just wanna say I'm sorry. I
never meant it to turn out like this.

1073
01:07:50,775 --> 01:07:52,860
I know. Me neither.

1074
01:07:55,446 --> 01:07:58,574
Tournament of Roses.
Roses can't do sports.

1075
01:07:59,825 --> 01:08:03,162
Wait a minute. Roses. Roses?

1076
01:08:03,662 --> 01:08:05,164
Roses!

1077
01:08:05,831 --> 01:08:07,666
Vanessa!

1078
01:08:08,334 --> 01:08:10,019
Roses?

1079
01:08:10,044 --> 01:08:11,353
Barry?

1080
01:08:11,378 --> 01:08:14,523
- Roses are flowers!
- Yes, they are.

1081
01:08:14,548 --> 01:08:17,568
Flowers, bees, pollen!

1082
01:08:17,593 --> 01:08:21,030
I know. That's why this
is the last parade.

1083
01:08:21,055 --> 01:08:25,101
Maybe not. Could you
ask him to slow down?

1084
01:08:25,309 --> 01:08:26,894
Could you slow down?

1085
01:08:29,688 --> 01:08:30,706
Barry!

1086
01:08:30,731 --> 01:08:34,752
Okay, I made a huge mistake. This
is a total disaster, all my fault.

1087
01:08:34,777 --> 01:08:36,170
Yes, it kind of is.

1088
01:08:36,195 --> 01:08:39,256
I've ruined the planet. I wanted
to help you with the flower shop.

1089
01:08:39,281 --> 01:08:40,925
I've made it worse.

1090
01:08:40,950 --> 01:08:43,427
Actually, it's
completely closed down.

1091
01:08:43,452 --> 01:08:45,429
I thought maybe you
were remodeling.

1092
01:08:45,454 --> 01:08:47,431
But I have another idea,

1093
01:08:47,456 --> 01:08:49,767
and it's greater than my
previous ideas combined.

1094
01:08:49,792 --> 01:08:51,585
I don't want to hear it!

1095
01:08:52,545 --> 01:08:56,565
All right, they have the roses,
the roses have the pollen.

1096
01:08:56,590 --> 01:08:59,860
I know every bee, plant and
flower bud in this park.

1097
01:08:59,885 --> 01:09:03,531
All we gotta do is get what they've
got back here with what we've got.

1098
01:09:03,556 --> 01:09:04,740
- Bees.
- Park.

1099
01:09:04,765 --> 01:09:05,950
- Pollen!
- Flowers.

1100
01:09:05,975 --> 01:09:08,435
- Repollination!
- Across the nation!

1101
01:09:09,687 --> 01:09:12,398
Tournament of Roses,
Pasadena, California.

1102
01:09:12,606 --> 01:09:15,668
They've got nothing but flowers,
floats and cotton candy.

1103
01:09:15,693 --> 01:09:17,044
Security will be tight.

1104
01:09:17,069 --> 01:09:18,879
L have an idea.

1105
01:09:18,904 --> 01:09:20,714
Vanessa Bloome, FTD.

1106
01:09:20,739 --> 01:09:22,992
Official floral business.
It's real.

1107
01:09:23,159 --> 01:09:25,594
Sorry, ma'am. Nice brooch.

1108
01:09:25,619 --> 01:09:27,121
Thank you. It was a gift.

1109
01:09:28,247 --> 01:09:30,583
Once inside, we just
pick the right float.

1110
01:09:32,251 --> 01:09:33,919
How about The
Princess and the Pea?

1111
01:09:34,295 --> 01:09:37,273
I could be the princess,
and you could be the pea!

1112
01:09:37,298 --> 01:09:38,591
Yes, I got it.

1113
01:09:40,509 --> 01:09:42,736
- Where should I sit?
- What are you?

1114
01:09:42,761 --> 01:09:44,655
- I believe I'm the pea.
- The pea?

1115
01:09:44,680 --> 01:09:46,407
It goes under the mattresses.

1116
01:09:46,432 --> 01:09:49,243
- Not in this fairy tale, sweetheart.
- I'm getting the marshal.

1117
01:09:49,268 --> 01:09:52,271
You do that! This whole
parade is a fiasco!

1118
01:09:54,523 --> 01:09:56,317
Let's see what this baby'll do.

1119
01:09:57,860 --> 01:09:59,820
Hey, what are you doing?

1120
01:10:01,280 --> 01:10:06,160
Then all we do is blend in with
traffic, without arousing suspicion.

1121
01:10:09,496 --> 01:10:12,266
Once at the airport,
there's no stopping us.

1122
01:10:12,291 --> 01:10:13,309
Stop! Security.

1123
01:10:13,334 --> 01:10:15,603
- You and your insect pack your float?
- Yes.

1124
01:10:15,628 --> 01:10:18,189
Has it been in your
possession the entire time?

1125
01:10:18,214 --> 01:10:20,566
Would you remove your shoes?

1126
01:10:20,591 --> 01:10:22,943
- Remove your stinger.
- It's part of me.

1127
01:10:22,968 --> 01:10:25,930
I know. Just having some fun.
Enjoy your flight.

1128
01:10:32,311 --> 01:10:36,148
Then if we're lucky, we'll have
just enough pollen to do the job.

1129
01:10:37,524 --> 01:10:39,001
Can you believe
how lucky we are?

1130
01:10:39,026 --> 01:10:41,170
We have just enough
pollen to do the job!

1131
01:10:41,195 --> 01:10:42,838
I think this is gonna work.

1132
01:10:42,863 --> 01:10:44,173
It's got to work.

1133
01:10:44,198 --> 01:10:46,675
Attention, passengers,
this is Captain Scott.

1134
01:10:46,700 --> 01:10:49,053
We have a bit of bad
weather in New York.

1135
01:10:49,078 --> 01:10:51,847
It looks like we'll experience
a couple hours delay.

1136
01:10:51,872 --> 01:10:54,516
Barry, these are cut
flowers with no water.

1137
01:10:54,541 --> 01:10:55,893
They'll never make it.

1138
01:10:55,918 --> 01:10:58,062
I gotta get up there
and talk to them.

1139
01:10:58,087 --> 01:10:59,380
Be careful.

1140
01:11:02,049 --> 01:11:04,568
Can I get help with the
Sky Mall magazine?

1141
01:11:04,593 --> 01:11:08,555
I'd like to order the talking
inflatable nose and ear hair trimmer.

1142
01:11:12,768 --> 01:11:14,578
Captain, I'm in a
real situation.

1143
01:11:14,603 --> 01:11:16,313
- What'd you say, Hal?
- Nothing.

1144
01:11:16,730 --> 01:11:18,123
Bee!

1145
01:11:18,148 --> 01:11:20,985
Don't freak out! My
entire species...

1146
01:11:25,572 --> 01:11:26,907
What are you doing?

1147
01:11:31,620 --> 01:11:34,932
- Wait a minute! I'm an attorney!
- Who's an attorney?

1148
01:11:34,957 --> 01:11:36,083
Don't move.

1149
01:11:41,171 --> 01:11:42,256
Oh, Barry.

1150
01:11:43,257 --> 01:11:46,068
Good afternoon, passengers.
This is your captain.

1151
01:11:46,093 --> 01:11:50,572
Would a Miss Vanessa Bloome in 24B
please report to the cockpit?

1152
01:11:50,597 --> 01:11:52,266
And please hurry!

1153
01:11:54,935 --> 01:11:56,412
What happened here?

1154
01:11:56,437 --> 01:11:59,790
There was a DustBuster, a
toupee, a life raft exploded.

1155
01:11:59,815 --> 01:12:02,943
One's bald, one's in a boat,
they're both unconscious!

1156
01:12:03,777 --> 01:12:05,546
- Is that another bee joke?
- No!

1157
01:12:05,571 --> 01:12:07,256
No one's flying the plane!

1158
01:12:07,281 --> 01:12:11,510
This is JFK control tower,
Flight 356. What's your status?

1159
01:12:11,535 --> 01:12:14,930
This is Vanessa Bloome. I'm
a florist from New York.

1160
01:12:14,955 --> 01:12:16,181
Where's the pilot?

1161
01:12:16,206 --> 01:12:18,767
He's unconscious, and
so is the copilot.

1162
01:12:18,792 --> 01:12:22,062
Not good. Does anyone onboard
have flight experience?

1163
01:12:22,087 --> 01:12:23,772
As a matter of fact, there is.

1164
01:12:23,797 --> 01:12:25,774
- Who's that?
- Barry Benson.

1165
01:12:25,799 --> 01:12:28,110
From the honey trial? Oh, great.

1166
01:12:28,135 --> 01:12:30,779
Vanessa, this is nothing
more than a big metal bee.

1167
01:12:30,804 --> 01:12:32,865
It's got giant
wings, huge engines.

1168
01:12:32,890 --> 01:12:34,241
I can't fly a plane.

1169
01:12:34,266 --> 01:12:36,785
- Why not? Isn't John Travolta a pilot?
- Yes.

1170
01:12:36,810 --> 01:12:38,520
How hard could it be?

1171
01:12:39,688 --> 01:12:42,399
Wait, Barry! We're headed
into some lightning.

1172
01:12:45,486 --> 01:12:49,548
This is Bob Bumble. We have some
late-breaking news from JFK Airport,

1173
01:12:49,573 --> 01:12:52,468
where a suspenseful
scene is developing.

1174
01:12:52,493 --> 01:12:55,220
Barry Benson, fresh from
his legal victory...

1175
01:12:55,245 --> 01:12:56,472
That's Barry!

1176
01:12:56,497 --> 01:13:00,017
Is attempting to land a plane,
loaded with people, flowers

1177
01:13:00,042 --> 01:13:02,311
and an incapacitated
flight crew.

1178
01:13:02,336 --> 01:13:03,479
Flowers?

1179
01:13:03,504 --> 01:13:07,858
We have a storm in the area and
two individuals at the controls

1180
01:13:07,883 --> 01:13:10,194
with absolutely no
flight experience.

1181
01:13:10,219 --> 01:13:13,155
Just a minute. There's
a bee on that plane.

1182
01:13:13,180 --> 01:13:17,493
I'm quite familiar with Mr. Benson
and his no-account compadres.

1183
01:13:17,518 --> 01:13:19,161
They've done enough damage.

1184
01:13:19,186 --> 01:13:21,121
But isn't he your only hope?

1185
01:13:21,146 --> 01:13:23,999
Technically, a bee shouldn't
be able to fly at all.

1186
01:13:24,024 --> 01:13:26,001
Their wings are too small...

1187
01:13:26,026 --> 01:13:28,420
Haven't we heard this
a million times?

1188
01:13:28,445 --> 01:13:32,216
"The surface area of the wings
and body mass make no sense."

1189
01:13:32,241 --> 01:13:34,009
- Get this on the air!
- Got it.

1190
01:13:34,034 --> 01:13:35,828
- Stand by.
- We're going live.

1191
01:13:36,870 --> 01:13:39,181
The way we work may
be a mystery to you.

1192
01:13:39,206 --> 01:13:43,143
Making honey takes a lot of
bees doing a lot of small jobs.

1193
01:13:43,168 --> 01:13:45,354
But let me tell you
about a small job.

1194
01:13:45,379 --> 01:13:48,273
If you do it well, it
makes a big difference.

1195
01:13:48,298 --> 01:13:51,276
More than we realized.
To us, to everyone.

1196
01:13:51,301 --> 01:13:55,197
That's why I want to get bees
back to working together.

1197
01:13:55,222 --> 01:13:56,824
That's the bee way!

1198
01:13:56,849 --> 01:14:00,202
We're not made of Jell-O.
We get behind a fellow.

1199
01:14:00,227 --> 01:14:02,563
- Black and yellow!
- Hello!

1200
01:14:06,483 --> 01:14:08,210
Left, right, down, hover.

1201
01:14:08,235 --> 01:14:10,045
- Hover?
- Forget hover.

1202
01:14:10,070 --> 01:14:12,614
This isn't so hard. Beep-beep!
Beep-beep!

1203
01:14:15,576 --> 01:14:17,386
Barry, what happened?

1204
01:14:17,411 --> 01:14:20,389
Wait, I think we were on
autopilot the whole time.

1205
01:14:20,414 --> 01:14:23,517
- That may have been helping me.
- And now we're not!

1206
01:14:23,542 --> 01:14:26,128
So it turns out I
cannot fly a plane.

1207
01:14:29,423 --> 01:14:33,844
All of you, let's get behind
this fellow! Move it out!

1208
01:14:34,261 --> 01:14:35,929
Move out!

1209
01:14:43,812 --> 01:14:45,914
Our only chance is
if I do what I'd do,

1210
01:14:45,939 --> 01:14:47,875
you copy me with the
wings of the plane!

1211
01:14:47,900 --> 01:14:48,959
Don't have to yell.

1212
01:14:48,984 --> 01:14:51,587
I'm not yelling! We're
in a lot of trouble.

1213
01:14:51,612 --> 01:14:55,257
It's very hard to concentrate with
that panicky tone in your voice!

1214
01:14:55,282 --> 01:14:58,118
It's not a tone. I'm panicking!

1215
01:15:08,879 --> 01:15:10,147
I can't do this!

1216
01:15:10,172 --> 01:15:13,775
Vanessa, pull yourself together.
You have to snap out of it!

1217
01:15:13,800 --> 01:15:15,027
You snap out of it.

1218
01:15:15,052 --> 01:15:16,195
You snap out of it.

1219
01:15:16,220 --> 01:15:18,530
- You snap out of it!
- You snap out of it!

1220
01:15:18,555 --> 01:15:21,141
- You snap out of it!
- You snap out of it!

1221
01:15:41,078 --> 01:15:43,597
- You snap out of it!
- You snap out of it!

1222
01:15:43,622 --> 01:15:45,974
- Hold it!
- Why? Come on, it's my turn.

1223
01:15:45,999 --> 01:15:48,126
How is the plane flying?

1224
01:15:48,502 --> 01:15:49,670
I don't know.

1225
01:15:50,504 --> 01:15:51,563
Hello?

1226
01:15:51,588 --> 01:15:54,733
Benson, got any flowers for
a happy occasion in there?

1227
01:15:54,758 --> 01:15:56,151
The Pollen Jocks!

1228
01:15:56,176 --> 01:15:58,153
They do get behind a fellow.

1229
01:15:58,178 --> 01:15:59,821
- Black and yellow.
- Hello.

1230
01:15:59,846 --> 01:16:03,158
All right, let's drop this
tin can on the blacktop.

1231
01:16:03,183 --> 01:16:05,994
Where? I can't see anything.
Can you?

1232
01:16:06,019 --> 01:16:08,330
No, nothing. It's all cloudy.

1233
01:16:08,355 --> 01:16:10,832
Come on. You got to
think bee, Barry.

1234
01:16:10,857 --> 01:16:12,834
- Thinking bee.
- Thinking bee.

1235
01:16:12,859 --> 01:16:16,171
Thinking bee! Thinking bee!
Thinking bee!

1236
01:16:16,196 --> 01:16:19,007
Wait a minute. I think
I'm feeling something.

1237
01:16:19,032 --> 01:16:21,802
- What?
- I don't know. It's strong, pulling me.

1238
01:16:21,827 --> 01:16:24,179
Like a 27-million-year-old
instinct.

1239
01:16:24,204 --> 01:16:25,539
Bring the nose down.

1240
01:16:28,458 --> 01:16:32,020
Thinking bee! Thinking bee!
Thinking bee!

1241
01:16:32,045 --> 01:16:36,191
- What in the world is on the tarmac?
- Get some lights on that!

1242
01:16:36,216 --> 01:16:40,112
Thinking bee! Thinking bee!
Thinking bee!

1243
01:16:40,137 --> 01:16:42,531
- Vanessa, aim for the flower.
- Okay.

1244
01:16:42,556 --> 01:16:44,032
Cut the engines.

1245
01:16:44,057 --> 01:16:46,702
We're going in on bee power.
Ready, boys?

1246
01:16:46,727 --> 01:16:47,894
Affirmative!

1247
01:16:50,731 --> 01:16:53,292
Good. Good. Easy, now.
That's it.

1248
01:16:53,317 --> 01:16:55,043
Land on that flower!

1249
01:16:55,068 --> 01:16:56,712
Ready? Full reverse!

1250
01:16:56,737 --> 01:16:57,904
Spin it around!

1251
01:17:01,074 --> 01:17:03,302
- Not that flower! The other one!
- Which one?

1252
01:17:03,327 --> 01:17:05,596
- That flower.
- I'm aiming at the flower!

1253
01:17:05,621 --> 01:17:10,309
That's a fat guy in a flowered shirt.
I mean the giant pulsating flower

1254
01:17:10,334 --> 01:17:13,228
made of millions of bees!

1255
01:17:13,253 --> 01:17:16,256
Pull forward. Nose down.
Tail up.

1256
01:17:16,923 --> 01:17:18,592
Rotate around it.

1257
01:17:19,092 --> 01:17:22,654
- This is insane, Barry!
- This is the only way I know how to fly.

1258
01:17:22,679 --> 01:17:26,742
Am I koo-koo-kachoo, or is this plane
flying in an insect-like pattern?

1259
01:17:26,767 --> 01:17:30,245
Get your nose in there. Don't be
afraid. Smell it. Full reverse!

1260
01:17:30,270 --> 01:17:32,314
Just drop it. Be a part of it.

1261
01:17:32,939 --> 01:17:37,736
Aim for the center! Now drop
it in! Drop it in, woman!

1262
01:17:48,288 --> 01:17:49,956
Come on, already.

1263
01:17:54,795 --> 01:17:58,273
Barry, we did it! You
taught me how to fly!

1264
01:17:58,298 --> 01:18:00,776
- Yes. No high-five!
- Right.

1265
01:18:00,801 --> 01:18:03,779
Barry, it worked! Did you
see the giant flower?

1266
01:18:03,804 --> 01:18:08,325
What giant flower? Where? Of course
I saw the flower! That was genius!

1267
01:18:08,350 --> 01:18:10,894
- Thank you.
- But we're not done yet.

1268
01:18:19,986 --> 01:18:21,296
Listen, everyone!

1269
01:18:21,321 --> 01:18:23,882
This runway is covered
with the last pollen

1270
01:18:23,907 --> 01:18:26,968
from the last flowers
available anywhere on Earth.

1271
01:18:26,993 --> 01:18:29,137
That means this is
our last chance.

1272
01:18:29,162 --> 01:18:34,334
We're the only ones who make honey,
pollinate flowers and dress like this.

1273
01:18:34,668 --> 01:18:38,021
If we're gonna survive as a
species, this is our moment!

1274
01:18:38,046 --> 01:18:39,731
What do you say?

1275
01:18:39,756 --> 01:18:44,736
Are we going to be bees, or just
Museum of Natural History keychains?

1276
01:18:44,761 --> 01:18:46,363
We're bees!

1277
01:18:46,388 --> 01:18:47,614
Keychain!

1278
01:18:47,639 --> 01:18:51,493
Then follow me! Except Keychain.

1279
01:18:51,518 --> 01:18:53,495
Hold on, Barry. Here.

1280
01:18:53,520 --> 01:18:55,021
You've earned this.

1281
01:19:01,862 --> 01:19:03,572
Yeah!

1282
01:19:06,366 --> 01:19:11,371
I'm a Pollen Jock! And it's a perfect
fit. All I gotta do are the sleeves.

1283
01:19:15,459 --> 01:19:16,852
Oh, yeah.

1284
01:19:16,877 --> 01:19:18,628
That's our Barry.

1285
01:19:22,048 --> 01:19:24,217
Here comes the sun.

1286
01:19:25,719 --> 01:19:27,446
Here comes the sun.

1287
01:19:27,471 --> 01:19:31,433
And I say, it's all right.

1288
01:19:34,227 --> 01:19:36,121
Little darling

1289
01:19:36,146 --> 01:19:40,192
lt's been a long,
cold, lonely winter.

1290
01:19:41,526 --> 01:19:47,073
Little darling lt feels like
years since it's been here.

1291
01:19:48,575 --> 01:19:50,577
Here comes the sun.

1292
01:19:52,162 --> 01:19:57,250
Here comes the sun And
I say, it's all right.

1293
01:20:04,674 --> 01:20:06,485
Little darling.

1294
01:20:06,510 --> 01:20:10,514
The smiles returning
to the faces.

1295
01:20:11,765 --> 01:20:13,141
Little darling

1296
01:20:13,600 --> 01:20:17,437
lt seems like years
since it's been here.

1297
01:20:19,105 --> 01:20:21,274
Here comes the sun.

1298
01:20:22,567 --> 01:20:24,461
Here comes the sun.

1299
01:20:24,486 --> 01:20:28,256
And I say, it's all right.

1300
01:20:28,281 --> 01:20:30,242
Mom! The bees are back!

1301
01:20:40,126 --> 01:20:43,271
Sun, sun, sun, here it comes!

1302
01:20:43,296 --> 01:20:45,607
If anybody needs to make
a call, now's the time.

1303
01:20:45,632 --> 01:20:48,093
I got a feeling we'll be
working late tonight!

1304
01:20:57,185 --> 01:21:02,457
Little darling I feel the
ice is slowly melting.

1305
01:21:02,482 --> 01:21:05,627
Here's your change.
Have a great afternoon!

1306
01:21:05,652 --> 01:21:07,320
Can I help who's next?

1307
01:21:07,654 --> 01:21:12,200
Would you like some honey with that? It
is bee-approved. Don't forget these.

1308
01:21:12,617 --> 01:21:16,788
Milk, cream, cheese, it's all
me. And I don't see a nickel!

1309
01:21:17,998 --> 01:21:20,976
Sometimes I just feel
like a piece of meat!

1310
01:21:21,001 --> 01:21:22,561
I had no idea.

1311
01:21:22,586 --> 01:21:24,855
Barry, I'm sorry. Have
you got a moment?

1312
01:21:24,880 --> 01:21:28,316
Would you excuse me? My mosquito
associate will help you.

1313
01:21:28,341 --> 01:21:29,401
Sorry I'm late.

1314
01:21:29,426 --> 01:21:30,610
He's a lawyer, too?

1315
01:21:30,635 --> 01:21:35,490
I was already a blood-sucking parasite.
All I needed was a briefcase.

1316
01:21:35,515 --> 01:21:37,409
Have a great afternoon!

1317
01:21:37,434 --> 01:21:41,663
Barry, I just got this huge tulip
order, and I can't get them anywhere.

1318
01:21:41,688 --> 01:21:44,124
No problem, Vannie.
Just leave it to me.

1319
01:21:44,149 --> 01:21:47,002
You're a lifesaver, Barry.
Can I help who's next?

1320
01:21:47,027 --> 01:21:50,171
All right, scramble, Jocks!
It's time to fly.

1321
01:21:50,196 --> 01:21:52,032
Thank you, Barry!

1322
01:21:54,784 --> 01:21:57,095
That bee is living my life!

1323
01:21:57,120 --> 01:21:58,305
Let it go, Kenny.

1324
01:21:58,330 --> 01:22:00,916
- When will this nightmare end?
- Let it all go.

1325
01:22:02,375 --> 01:22:05,770
Here comes the sun.

1326
01:22:05,795 --> 01:22:07,188
Here comes the sun.

1327
01:22:07,213 --> 01:22:09,524
- Beautiful day to fly.
- Sure is.

1328
01:22:09,549 --> 01:22:13,178
Between you and me, I was dying
to get out of that office.

1329
01:22:15,013 --> 01:22:17,057
It's all right.

1330
01:29:42,502 --> 01:29:45,355
You have got to start
thinking bee, my friend.

1331
01:29:45,380 --> 01:29:47,231
- Thinking bee!
- Me?

1332
01:29:47,256 --> 01:29:51,319
Thinking bee! Thinking bee! Get
smart and start thinking bee!

1333
01:29:51,344 --> 01:29:52,362
Gee!

1334
01:29:52,387 --> 01:29:54,906
- Flying here and buzzing there.
- I'm loving the views.

1335
01:29:54,931 --> 01:29:59,185
Listen to me, cousin Every
buzzer must choose to be a bee.

1336
01:29:59,852 --> 01:30:02,580
- Or not to be.
- Start thinking bee.

1337
01:30:02,605 --> 01:30:05,583
- Barry, you got no occupation.
- You mean like pollination?

1338
01:30:05,608 --> 01:30:08,252
- Hey, now, that's thinking bee!
- Start thinking bee.

1339
01:30:08,277 --> 01:30:10,505
Listen, fella Ain't
you been on the tour?

1340
01:30:10,530 --> 01:30:13,341
- Your stripes are black and yella.
- L just want to be sure.

1341
01:30:13,366 --> 01:30:15,843
- To be a bee.
- Start thinking bee.

1342
01:30:15,868 --> 01:30:18,679
- Can't I wait and see?
- No, Barry, that's not to be.

1343
01:30:18,704 --> 01:30:21,599
Be a busy little bee Not
a tizzy little bee.

1344
01:30:21,624 --> 01:30:24,685
Hold it. Let's just stop
for a second. Hold it.

1345
01:30:24,710 --> 01:30:28,356
I'm sorry. I'm sorry, everyone.
Can we stop here?

1346
01:30:28,381 --> 01:30:31,859
I'm not making a major life decision
during a production number!

1347
01:30:31,884 --> 01:30:35,029
All right. Take ten, everybody.
Wrap it up, guys.

1348
01:30:35,054 --> 01:30:37,181
L had virtually no
rehearsal for that.

